PROGRESSIVE_THEORY_DICT = {('EM', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            1.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Propositions & The 5 Core Logic Connectives (Complete Truth Table)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>What is a Proposition?</b> A declarative sentence that is either strictly True (T) or strictly False (F), but never both.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- \'5 is a prime number\' is a proposition (True).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- \'Please close the door\' is NOT a proposition (command).<br/><br/>• <b>Complete Master Truth Table for the 5 Operators:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>P   Q   ~P (NOT)   P ∧ Q (AND)   P ∨ Q (OR)   P → Q (Implication)   P ↔ Q (Biconditional)<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>T   T      F           T             T                 T                       T<br/>T   F      F           F             T                 F (Broken promise!)     F<br/>F   T      T           F             T                 T (Vacuously true)      F<br/>F   F      T           F             F                 T (Vacuously true)      T</div>• <b>Crucial Implication Identity:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>(P → Q) ≡ (~P ∨ Q)</code> (Use this identity to rewrite implications in 99% of GATE proofs!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: P → Q is False ONLY when P = True and Q = False! When P is False, (P → Q) is 100% GUARANTEED to be True regardless of Q (Vacuous Truth).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            1.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • For any conditional statement <b>P → Q</b> (\'If P, then Q\'):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Contrapositive (~Q → ~P):</b> \'If NOT Q, then NOT P\'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>100% Logically Equivalent:</b> <code>(P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Proving the contrapositive proves the original statement!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Converse (Q → P):</b> \'If Q, then P\'. NOT logically equivalent to P → Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Inverse (~P → ~Q):</b> \'If NOT P, then NOT Q\'. Equivalent to the Converse, NOT the original.<br/><br/>• <b>Example:</b> \'If it rains (P), the grass is wet (Q)\':<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Contrapositive: \'If the grass is NOT wet (~Q), it did NOT rain (~P)\' (True!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Converse: \'If grass is wet, it rained\' (False! A sprinkler could have caused it).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P). Converse (Q → P) and Inverse (~P → ~Q) are logically equivalent to EACH OTHER, but NEVER to the original statement!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            1.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            First-Order Logic: Quantifiers (∀ vs ∃) & De Morgan\'s Rules\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Universal Quantifier (∀x):</b> \'For all x\'. True only if P(x) holds for EVERY single element in the universe.<br/>• <b>Existential Quantifier (∃x):</b> \'There exists an x\'. True if at least ONE element makes P(x) true.<br/><br/>• <b>De Morgan\'s Negation Rules for Quantifiers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <code>~[ ∀x P(x) ] ≡ ∃x [ ~P(x) ]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Negation of \'All students passed\' is \'There is at least one student who did NOT pass\'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <code>~[ ∃x P(x) ] ≡ ∀x [ ~P(x) ]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Negation of \'Someone scored 100\' is \'Everyone scored not 100\'.<br/><br/>• <b>Order of Quantifiers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>∀x ∃y P(x, y)</code> (Everyone loves someone) is NOT equal to <code>∃y ∀x P(x, y)</code> (There is one specific person whom everybody loves!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Negating \'All X are Y\' is \'There exists an X that is NOT Y\'! It is NEVER \'No X are Y\'. Negating ∀ gives ∃, and negating ∃ gives ∀.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            1.4\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Relations & Properties (Counting Formulas for Set of Size n)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Let set A contain <b>n</b> elements. The Cartesian product A × A contains <b>n²</b> ordered pairs.<br/>• <b>The 5 Fundamental Counting Formulas to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>1. Total Relations:               2^(n²)<br/>2. Reflexive Relations:           2^(n² - n)<br/>3. Symmetric Relations:           2^(n(n + 1) / 2)<br/>4. Reflexive & Symmetric:         2^(n(n - 1) / 2)<br/>5. Equivalence Relations:         Bell Numbers B(n): B(1)=1, B(2)=2, B(3)=5, B(4)=15, B(5)=52</div>• <b>Equivalence Relation Definition:</b> A relation that is simultaneously <b>Reflexive</b>, <b>Symmetric</b>, and <b>Transitive</b>. It partitions the set into disjoint Equivalence Classes.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a set with 4 elements, total equivalence relations = Bell Number B(4) = 15! Reflexive relations = 2^(16 - 4) = 2^12 = 4096.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Counting Symmetric Relations\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Set A has 4 elements: A = {1, 2, 3, 4}. How many symmetric relations can be formed on set A?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Formula for symmetric relations on a set of size n is: Total = 2^(n(n+1)/2).<br/>• Step 2: Here n = 4.<br/>• Step 3: Exponent = (4 × (4 + 1)) / 2 = (4 × 5) / 2 = 20 / 2 = 10.<br/>• Step 4: Compute 2^10 = 1024.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total Symmetric Relations = 1024.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          A relation can be BOTH symmetric and anti-symmetric at the same time (e.g. Identity relation on any set)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Empty relation on a non-empty set is NOT reflexive, but it is vacuously symmetric and transitive!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Strict Courtroom Contract Clause\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Proposition: Like entering a Wi-Fi password—it is strictly True or False. Sentences like \'What time is it?\' are not propositions.\n• Implication (P -> Q): Dad promises \'If you score AIR 1 (P), I will buy you a bike (Q)\'. He only breaks his word if you get AIR 1 and he refuses to buy the bike (T -> F = False). If you do not get AIR 1, whatever happens never violates the promise (Vacuous Truth)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            2.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Pigeonhole Principle (PHP) & Guaranteed Matches\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Basic Pigeonhole Principle:</b> If <code>k + 1</code> or more objects are placed into <code>k</code> boxes, then at least one box must contain 2 or more objects.<br/>• <b>Generalized PHP:</b> If <code>N</code> objects are placed into <code>k</code> boxes, at least one box contains at least <b>⌈N / k⌉</b> objects.<br/>• <b>Inverse PHP Question (Common in GATE):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;To guarantee that at least <b>m</b> items land in the same box out of <b>k</b> boxes:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Minimum Items Needed = k · (m - 1) + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>Example:</i> To guarantee 5 cards of the same suit (4 suits): 4 · (5 - 1) + 1 = 17 cards.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In guaranteed match questions: Worst-case scenario is having (m - 1) items in every single box. Adding 1 more item triggers the guarantee: N_min = k(m - 1) + 1!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            2.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Graph Theory: Handshaking Theorem & Planar Graphs\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Handshaking Lemma:</b> In any undirected graph: <b>∑ deg(v) = 2|E|</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Corollary:</i> In every graph, the number of vertices with ODD degree is ALWAYS EVEN!<br/>• <b>Complete Graph (K_n):</b> Total edges = <b>n(n - 1) / 2</b>.<br/>• <b>Bipartite Graph:</b> Graph is bipartite IF AND ONLY IF it has <b>NO odd-length cycles</b> (2-colorable).<br/><br/>• <b>Planar Graphs & Euler\'s Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>V - E + R = 2</b> (where R is total regions/faces including the infinite exterior face).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Maximum edges in any simple connected planar graph: <b>E ≤ 3V - 6</b> (for V ≥ 3).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If triangle-free (no C_3 cycle): <b>E ≤ 2V - 4</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: K_5 (complete graph on 5 vertices) and K_{3,3} (complete bipartite utility graph) are the two minimal non-planar graphs (Kuratowski\'s Theorem)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Planar Graph Faces Calculation\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A connected planar graph has 8 vertices and each vertex has degree 3. How many faces (regions) does it divide the plane into?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Calculate total edges using Handshaking Theorem: ∑ deg(v) = 2E.<br/>• Step 2: 8 vertices with degree 3 ⇒ Total degree sum = 8 × 3 = 24.<br/>• Step 3: 2E = 24 ⇒ E = 12 edges.<br/>• Step 4: Use Euler\'s planar formula: V - E + R = 2.<br/>• Step 5: Substitute V = 8, E = 12: 8 - 12 + R = 2 ⇒ -4 + R = 2 ⇒ R = 6.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total regions R = 6 (5 bounded faces + 1 unbounded outer face).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Euler\'s formula V - E + R = 2 requires the graph to be CONNECTED. If disconnected with k components: V - E + R = 1 + k.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Sum of degrees in any graph is ALWAYS an even number. If an exam question presents degree sequence with an odd sum, such a graph CANNOT exist!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Sock Drawer in the Dark & Party Handshakes\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Pigeonhole Principle: If a drawer has black and brown socks, pulling 3 socks guarantees at least one matching pair because 3 socks go into 2 color buckets.\n• Handshaking Theorem: At any party, every handshake involves 2 people. So if you sum all individual handshakes counted by each person, the sum is ALWAYS an even number!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            3.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            System of Linear Equations (AX = B) Consistency\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Consider system <b>AX = B</b> with n variables. Let [A | B] be the augmented matrix.<br/>• <b>Step-by-Step Consistency Test:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Inconsistent (No Solution):</b> <code>Rank(A) ≠ Rank(A | B)</code> (i.e. Rank(A) < Rank(A | B)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Unique Solution:</b> <code>Rank(A) == Rank(A | B) == n</code> (Full rank equal to number of unknowns).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Infinitely Many Solutions:</b> <code>Rank(A) == Rank(A | B) = r < n</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of linearly independent free variables = <b>n - r</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Homogeneous System (AX = 0):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Always consistent (trivial solution X = 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Has non-zero (non-trivial) solutions IF AND ONLY IF <b>det(A) = 0</b> (Rank(A) < n).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Homogeneous system AX = 0 has non-trivial solutions IF AND ONLY IF det(A) = 0. For non-homogeneous AX = B, check Rank(A) vs Rank(A|B)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            3.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Eigenvalues & Eigenvectors: The 5 Golden Properties\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Characteristic Equation: <code>det(A - λI) = 0</code>.<br/>• <b>The 5 Golden Properties for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Sum of Eigenvalues = Trace of A</b> (sum of main diagonal elements): <code>∑ λ_i = Tr(A)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Product of Eigenvalues = Determinant of A</b>: <code>∏ λ_i = det(A)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Triangular / Diagonal Matrices:</b> Eigenvalues are SIMPLY the main diagonal entries!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. If λ is an eigenvalue of A:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^k has eigenvalue <b>λ^k</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^(-1) has eigenvalue <b>1 / λ</b> (provided λ ≠ 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- (A + cI) has eigenvalue <b>λ + c</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Real Symmetric Matrices:</b> Always have strictly REAL eigenvalues.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 90% of GATE eigenvalue questions, DO NOT solve the cubic equation! Just verify Trace = Sum(λ) and Det = Product(λ) against the 4 options to find the answer in 10 seconds!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Finding Eigenvalues using Trace and Det\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Find the eigenvalues of A = [[3, 2], [1, 4]].\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Calculate Trace(A) = sum of main diagonal = 3 + 4 = 7.<br/>• Step 2: Calculate Det(A) = (3 × 4) - (2 × 1) = 12 - 2 = 10.<br/>• Step 3: Sum of eigenvalues λ1 + λ2 = 7 and Product λ1 · λ2 = 10.<br/>• Step 4: Two numbers that add to 7 and multiply to 10 are 2 and 5.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Eigenvalues are λ1 = 2, λ2 = 5.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          det(A + B) is NOT det(A) + det(B)! However, det(A × B) = det(A) × det(B).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Scaling an n × n matrix by constant k scales determinant by k^n: det(k·A) = k^n · det(A)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: 3D Video Game Graphics & Bridge Resonance\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Matrices: In 3D games, moving and rotating a character is simply multiplying 3D coordinate vectors by 4x4 transformation matrices.\n• Eigenvalues: The natural resonance frequencies of a bridge or skyscraper. If earthquake vibrations match an eigenvalue frequency, catastrophic collapse happens!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            4.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Bayes\' Theorem & Conditional Probability\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Conditional Probability Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Independent Events:</b> Events A and B are independent if and only if <b>P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Bayes\' Rule Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>P(A_i | B) = [ P(B | A_i) · P(A_i) ] / ∑ [ P(B | A_j) · P(A_j) ]</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Where the denominator is the Law of Total Probability P(B).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Mutually exclusive means they cannot occur together (P(A ∩ B) = 0). Independent means occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. They are completely different concepts!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            4.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Probability Distributions: Binomial, Poisson & Normal\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. Binomial Distribution B(n, p):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- P(X = k) = C(n, k) · p^k · (1 - p)^{n - k}.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Mean = <b>n · p</b>; Variance = <b>n · p · (1 - p)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>2. Poisson Distribution (Rate λ):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Models rare events: P(X = k) = (e^(-λ) · λ^k) / k!.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Unique Property:</b> Mean = <b>λ</b> and Variance = <b>λ</b> (Mean == Variance!).<br/><br/>• <b>3. Uniform Distribution on [a, b]:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Mean = <b>(a + b) / 2</b>; Variance = <b>(b - a)² / 12</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Poisson distribution, Mean = Variance = λ! If a question states \'the variance of a Poisson variable is 9\', then immediately mean = 9 and λ = 9!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Bayes\' Theorem in Factory Production\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Factory A produces 70% of items with 1% defect rate. Factory B produces 30% of items with 5% defect rate. An item chosen at random is defective. What is the probability it was made by Factory B?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: P(A) = 0.70, P(B) = 0.30.<br/>• Step 2: P(D | A) = 0.01, P(D | B) = 0.05.<br/>• Step 3: Total Probability P(D) = (0.70 × 0.01) + (0.30 × 0.05) = 0.007 + 0.015 = 0.022.<br/>• Step 4: By Bayes\' Theorem: P(B | D) = [ P(B) · P(D | B) ] / P(D) = 0.015 / 0.022 = 15 / 22 ≈ 0.6818.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ P(Factory B | Defective) = 15 / 22 (approx 68.18%).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Variance can NEVER be negative! Var(X) = E[X²] - (E[X])² ≥ 0.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Scaling rule: Var(aX + b) = a² · Var(X). The constant b adds 0 to variance!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Rare Disease Medical Test (Bayes\' Paradox)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Bayes\' Theorem: A medical test is 99% accurate, but a disease affects only 1 in 10,000 people. If you test positive, what is the chance you actually have the disease? Surprisingly, only ~1%! Because false positives from the 9,999 healthy people vastly outnumber true positives from the 1 sick person.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            5.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Signed Number Ranges & 2\'s Complement Arithmetic\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>For an n-bit binary representation:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Representation    Range for n bits                Number of Zeros   Special Notes<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Unsigned          0 to 2^n - 1                    1 (00...0)        All bits represent magnitude<br/>Sign-Magnitude    -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1) 2 (+0 and -0)    MSB is sign bit<br/>1\'s Complement    -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1) 2 (+0 and -0)    Bitwise inversion<br/>2\'s Complement    -2^(n-1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)      1 (Unique zero!)  Standard in all modern CPUs</div>• <b>Asymmetry in 2\'s Complement:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For n = 8 bits: Range is <b>-128 to +127</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Notice that <b>-128 has NO positive counterpart</b> in 8 bits! Attempting to negate -128 causes integer overflow.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 2\'s complement with n bits: The minimum negative number is -2^(n-1). It has no positive counterpart! Fast 2\'s complement shortcut: Copy bits from right to left up to and including the first \'1\', then flip all remaining bits to the left!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            5.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            IEEE-754 Single Precision 32-bit Floating Point\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Bit Fields: [ Sign (1 bit) | Biased Exponent E (8 bits) | Mantissa M (23 bits) ]</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Field          Bit Count   Function<br/>--------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Sign (S)       1 bit       0 for Positive, 1 for Negative<br/>Exponent (E)   8 bits      Excess-127 Bias: Stored E = actual exponent e + 127<br/>Mantissa (M)   23 bits     Fractional part with implicit leading 1: 1.M</div>• <b>Value Formula:</b> <code>(-1)^S × (1.M) × 2^(E - 127)</code>.<br/>• <b>Special Patterns:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 255 and M = 0: Represents <b>±Infinity</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 255 and M ≠ 0: Represents <b>NaN (Not a Number)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 0 and M = 0: Represents <b>±0</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The leading \'1\' in normalized floating point (1.M) is NEVER stored in memory—it is implicit! This gives 24 bits of precision from 23 stored mantissa bits.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: IEEE-754 Representation of -13.625\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Convert -13.625 into IEEE-754 32-bit hexadecimal format.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Sign is negative ⇒ S = 1.<br/>• Step 2: 13.625 in binary: 13 = 1101; 0.625 = 0.101 ⇒ 1101.101.<br/>• Step 3: Normalize: 1101.101 = 1.101101 × 2^3.<br/>• Step 4: Actual exponent e = 3 ⇒ Stored E = 3 + 127 = 130 = 10000010 in binary.<br/>• Step 5: Mantissa M = 101101 followed by 17 zeros.<br/>• Step 6: Combine: 1 | 10000010 | 10110100000000000000000. In Hex: 0xC15A0000.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Hexadecimal value = 0xC15A0000.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In IEEE-754, the smallest positive normalized number is 1.0 × 2^(-126) (where E = 1, not E = 0!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Double precision IEEE-754 uses 64 bits: 1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits (bias = 1023), and 52 mantissa bits.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Odometer Rollback & Game High Score Overflow\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • 2\'s Complement: Like a car odometer rolling backward from 00000 to 99999. In an 8-bit binary computer, rolling backward from 00000000 gives 11111111, which represents -1!\n• Overflow: In early arcade games (like Pac-Man level 256), adding 1 to 255 caused the 8-bit byte to overflow back to 0, breaking the game.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            6.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Boolean Laws & The Consensus Theorem\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Consensus Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AB + A\'C + BC = AB + A\'C</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Variable A appears in true form and complemented form (A, A\').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The term <b>BC</b> is redundant (consensus) and can be completely deleted!<br/><br/>• <b>Minimum Gate Counts for Universal Gates:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Target Gate   NAND Gates Required   NOR Gates Required<br/>-------------------------------------------------------<br/>NOT                  1                     1<br/>AND                  2                     3<br/>OR                   3                     2<br/>XOR                  4                     5<br/>XNOR                 5                     4</div>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: XOR requires 4 NAND gates (or 5 NOR gates). XNOR requires 5 NAND gates (or 4 NOR gates). Notice the perfect reciprocal symmetry: 4 vs 5!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            6.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Karnaugh Maps (K-Maps), Prime & Essential Prime Implicants\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Implicant:</b> Any product term that covers one or more 1s.<br/>• <b>Prime Implicant (PI):</b> A rectangular group of size 2^k (1, 2, 4, 8) that CANNOT be merged into a larger group.<br/>• <b>Essential Prime Implicant (EPI):</b> A Prime Implicant that covers at least one \'1\' that is NOT covered by any other prime implicant.<br/>• <b>Minimal SOP Formula:</b> MUST include ALL Essential Prime Implicants!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every EPI is a PI, but not every PI is an EPI! To find EPIs fast: look for a \'1\' that belongs to only ONE valid largest rectangle.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Boolean Function Minimization\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Simplify F = AB + A\'C + BC using Boolean laws.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check terms: AB, A\'C, BC.<br/>• Step 2: Variable A appears complemented (A and A\').<br/>• Step 3: The third term BC is the product of remaining variables.<br/>• Step 4: By Consensus Theorem, BC is redundant and can be deleted.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Simplified function: F = AB + A\'C.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In K-maps, grouping 0s gives the minimized POS expression (F\'), not SOP!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Don\'t care terms (X) can be included to enlarge groups, but you are NEVER required to cover all don\'t cares.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Universal LEGO Brick & Map Minimizer\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Universal Gates: Just as LEGO bricks can build any spaceship, NAND and NOR gates alone can build any computer circuit.\n• K-Map: A 2D puzzle where adjacent boxes differ by only 1 bit. Grouping 1s cancels out changing variables, shrinking circuit size.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            7.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Multiplexer (MUX) as a Universal Logic Module\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • A <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b> has 2^n data inputs, n select lines, and 1 output.<br/>• <b>Function Implementation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Using a <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b>: Connect all n variables directly to select lines. Connect truth table outputs (0 or 1) to data inputs. No extra gates needed!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Using a <b>2^(n-1) : 1 MUX</b>: Connect (n - 1) variables to select lines. The last variable X can provide data inputs as <b>0, 1, X, or X\'</b>. May need an inverter for X\'.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any Boolean function of n variables can be implemented using a single 2^(n-1) : 1 MUX with at most one external NOT gate!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            7.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Half Adder vs Full Adder Architecture\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Half Adder (HA):</b> Adds 2 bits A and B.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <code>A ⊕ B</code>; Carry = <code>A · B</code>.<br/>• <b>Full Adder (FA):</b> Adds 3 bits: A, B, and Carry-in C_in.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <code>A ⊕ B ⊕ C_in</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry-out = <code>AB + C_in(A ⊕ B)</code>.<br/>• <b>Hardware Construction:</b> 1 Full Adder = <b>2 Half Adders + 1 OR gate</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To build an n-bit Ripple Carry Adder, you need 1 Half Adder and (n - 1) Full Adders. Total delay = n × (carry propagation delay).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Implementing Logic with 4:1 MUX\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Implement F(A, B, C) = ∑m(1, 2, 4, 7) using a 4:1 MUX with select lines S1 = A, S0 = B.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: For AB = 00: m0(C=0)->0, m1(C=1)->1 ⇒ I0 = C.<br/>• Step 2: For AB = 01: m2(C=0)->1, m3(C=1)->0 ⇒ I1 = C\'.<br/>• Step 3: For AB = 10: m4(C=0)->1, m5(C=1)->0 ⇒ I2 = C\'.<br/>• Step 4: For AB = 11: m6(C=0)->0, m7(C=1)->1 ⇒ I3 = C.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Inputs to 4:1 MUX: I0 = C, I1 = C\', I2 = C\', I3 = C.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Pay strict attention to MSB vs LSB on select lines (S1 = A, S0 = B vs S1 = B, S0 = A)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Active-low enables in decoders require invert logic before cascading.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Train Track Switch & Letter Mail Sorter\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Multiplexer (MUX): Like a railway switch choosing which incoming track connects to the single outgoing main line, controlled by switch levers (select lines).\n• Decoder: A mail sorter reading a zip code and directing the parcel to exactly one specific delivery box.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            8.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Flip-Flop Characteristic Equations\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>JK Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = J·Q\' + K\'·Q</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=0: Hold (Q)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=1: Reset (0)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=0: Set (1)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=1: Toggle (Q\') - eliminates SR invalid state!<br/>• <b>D Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = D</code> (data storage).<br/>• <b>T Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = T ⊕ Q</code> (T=0: Hold, T=1: Toggle).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The characteristic equations must be on your fingertips: JK: Q_{next} = J·Q\' + K\'·Q | D: Q_{next} = D | T: Q_{next} = T ⊕ Q.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            8.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Setup Time, Hold Time & Max Clock Frequency\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Setup Time (t_setup):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable BEFORE active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Hold Time (t_hold):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable AFTER active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Minimum Clock Period (T_clk):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>T_clk ≥ t_{cq} + t_{comb} + t_{setup}</b>.<br/>• <b>Maximum Clock Frequency:</b> <code>f_max = 1 / T_clk_min</code>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To avoid Hold time violations: t_{cq_min} + t_{comb_min} ≥ t_{hold}. Notice that hold time is independent of clock frequency!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            8.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Shift Register Counters: Ring vs Johnson\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>For an n-flip-flop register:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Binary Counter:</b> Has <b>2^n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Ring Counter:</b> Output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Johnson (Twisted Ring) Counter:</b> Inverted output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>2n</b> unique states!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An n-flip-flop Johnson counter has 2n states, while an n-flip-flop Ring counter has only n states! Unused states: Ring has (2^n - n) unused states; Johnson has (2^n - 2n) unused states.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Maximum Clock Frequency\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A sequential circuit has flip-flop t_cq = 2 ns, t_setup = 1.5 ns, and combinational logic delay = 4.5 ns. What is the maximum clock frequency f_max?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Formula for minimum clock period: T_clk ≥ t_cq + t_comb + t_setup.<br/>• Step 2: T_clk_min = 2 ns + 4.5 ns + 1.5 ns = 8 ns.<br/>• Step 3: Maximum frequency f_max = 1 / T_clk_min = 1 / (8 × 10^(-9) s) = 125 MHz.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Maximum clock frequency = 125 MHz.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In negative edge-triggered ripple counter: connecting Q to clock gives an UP counter; connecting Q\' gives a DOWN counter!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Race-around condition in JK flip-flop occurs when J = 1, K = 1 and clock pulse width t_p > propagation delay t_pd in level-triggered latches.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: High-Speed Camera Snapshot & Subway Turnstile\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Flip-Flop: A high-speed camera taking a snapshot strictly on the rising edge of the photographer\'s flash. Inputs must stay stable before (setup time) and after (hold time) the flash.\n• Counter: A subway turnstile clicker that increments each time a passenger passes through, cycling back to zero when reaching its maximum count.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            9.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Effective Address (EA) & Memory References in Addressing Modes\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>What is Effective Address (EA)?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The actual physical memory address where the operand\'s data is stored in RAM.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Different addressing modes calculate EA in different ways to give programmers flexibility and speed.<br/><br/>• <b>Summary Table of Addressing Modes & Memory References:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Mode              Effective Address (EA)    Operand Location   Memory Accesses (Fetch + Execute)<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Immediate         No EA (part of opcode)    Inside Instruction 1 access (Fetch only)<br/>Direct (Absolute) EA = Address Field        M[Address Field]   2 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Read)<br/>Indirect          EA = M[Address Field]     M[M[Address]]      3 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Ptr + 1 Read)<br/>Register          No EA (CPU Register)      Register R         1 access (Fetch only - very fast!)<br/>Register Indirect EA = [Register R]         M[[Register R]]    2 accesses (1 Fetch + 1 Read)<br/>PC-Relative       EA = PC + Offset          M[PC + Offset]     Used in Jumps/Branches<br/>Indexed / Base    EA = Base Reg + Index Reg M[Base + Index]    Used in Array Traversals</div>• <b>Step-by-Step Example of Indirect Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Suppose instruction <code>LOAD @500</code> is fetched from memory address 1000:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <i>Step 1 (Instruction Fetch):</i> CPU accesses address 1000 to read <code>LOAD @500</code> (1st memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <i>Step 2 (Pointer Fetch):</i> CPU reads memory at address 500. Memory location 500 contains the number 800 (2nd memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <i>Step 3 (Operand Fetch):</i> CPU reads memory at address 800 to get the actual data value 42 (3rd memory access).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total memory accesses = 3.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In PC-Relative mode, PC is ALWAYS incremented to point to the next instruction BEFORE adding the offset: EA = (PC + 1 or PC + 2) + Offset. Watch out for word-length vs byte-length PC increments!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            9.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Instruction Word Formats & Expanding Opcode Technique\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Why Expanding Opcodes?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- An instruction has a fixed bit width (e.g. 16 bits).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If some instructions need 3 register addresses and others need only 1, we can shorten or expand the opcode bits so no bits are wasted.<br/><br/>• <b>Step-by-Step Bit Budget Calculation:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Consider a 16-bit instruction format with 16 CPU registers (requires 4 bits per register since 2^4 = 16):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>3-Register Instruction:</b> [ Opcode (4 bits) | Reg1 (4) | Reg2 (4) | Reg3 (4) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Total 4-bit opcode patterns = 2^4 = 16. If we allocate 14 patterns for 3-register instructions, <b>2 patterns remain free</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>2-Register Instruction:</b> [ Opcode (8 bits) | Reg1 (4) | Reg2 (4) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The 2 unused patterns expand across the 4 extra bits: <b>2 × 2^4 = 32 instructions possible</b>!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Expanding Opcode Formula: If k patterns are left unused at the higher level, they multiply by 2^(extra bits freed) at the next level: New Instructions = (Unused Patterns) × 2^(Freed Bits) - Allocated.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            9.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            RISC vs CISC Architecture Differences\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Simple, uniform 1-cycle instructions.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Strict Load/Store Architecture:</b> Arithmetic operations (ADD, SUB) can ONLY operate on CPU registers, NEVER directly on RAM!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Hardwired control unit (faster), large register file, heavily pipelined.<br/>• <b>CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Variable length, multi-cycle complex instructions (e.g. <code>ADD [1000], [2000]</code> directly adds memory locations).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Microprogrammed control unit with fewer general-purpose registers.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In RISC, ALU instructions CANNOT access memory! To add a memory variable to a register, you must do: LOAD R1, [Memory] -> ADD R2, R2, R1.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Calculating Memory References in Addressing Modes\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> An instruction stored at memory address 2000 has opcode and address field 500. Memory at 500 contains 900, and memory at 900 contains operand 75. How many total memory cycles are executed to fetch and run this instruction in Indirect Addressing Mode?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Fetch instruction from PC address 2000: 1 memory read cycle.<br/>• Step 2: Decode instruction: mode is Indirect. Read address field 500 to get pointer: 1 memory read cycle (returns 900).<br/>• Step 3: Read effective address 900 to get operand 75: 1 memory read cycle.<br/>• Step 4: Total memory references = 1 (fetch) + 1 (pointer fetch) + 1 (operand read) = 3 cycles.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total memory references = 3.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Do not forget to count the 1st memory reference needed to fetch the instruction itself!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Immediate addressing requires 1 memory reference for instruction fetch, but 0 references for operand fetch.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Four Ways to Find Treasure\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Immediate: Having $50 cash right inside your wallet (operand is inside the instruction itself).\n• Direct: Looking at an address slip that says \'Go to Locker 405\' (address field points straight to data in RAM).\n• Indirect: Looking at a slip that says \'Go to Desk 12 to find the key for Locker 405\' (a pointer to a pointer).\n• Indexed / Base: Your room is \'Building B, Room 14\' (Base Address of Building B + Room Offset 14).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            10.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Booth\'s Multiplication Algorithm for Signed 2\'s Complement\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Why Booth\'s Algorithm?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Standard binary multiplication fails for negative signed numbers.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Booth\'s algorithm works directly on 2\'s complement numbers and speeds up multiplication by skipping runs of consecutive 1s.<br/><br/>• <b>The Decision Table (Inspect bit pair Q_0 and Q_{-1}):</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Q_0  Q_{-1}   Meaning             Action Taken<br/>--------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/> 0     0     Run of zeros        Only Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR)<br/> 0     1     End of run of 1s    Accumulator A = A + Multiplicand M, then ASR<br/> 1     0     Start of run of 1s  Accumulator A = A - Multiplicand M, then ASR<br/> 1     1     Run of ones         Only Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR)</div>• <b>Crucial: Arithmetic Shift Right (ASR) Rule:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Shift all bits to the right by 1 position.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The Most Significant Bit (MSB, Sign Bit) is <b>REPLICATED</b> (kept identical to preserve the negative sign!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Worst case for Booth\'s algorithm is alternating bits like 01010101 (requires n additions/subtractions). Best case is all 1s (11111111) or all 0s (only 1 addition/subtraction or 0)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            10.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control Units\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Hardwired Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Built using logic gates, flip-flops, and decoders.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Pros:</b> Maximum possible speed. Used in high-performance RISC CPUs.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Cons:</b> Impossible to modify or add instructions without replacing physical chips.<br/><br/>• <b>Microprogrammed Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Control signals are stored as micro-instructions inside a Control Memory (ROM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Horizontal Microprogramming:</b> 1 bit per control signal. Fast, unencoded, but control words are very wide.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Vertical Microprogramming:</b> Signals are grouped into mutually exclusive fields. If a field has k signals, it requires <b>⌈log2(k + 1)⌉ bits</b> (the +1 is for the NO-OP inactive state!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In vertical microprogramming, always add 1 for the inactive state before taking log2: Bits = ⌈log2(Number of Signals + 1)⌉!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            10.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            DMA (Direct Memory Access) Data Transfer & Cycle Stealing\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>What is DMA?</b> Allows I/O devices (hard drives, NICs) to transfer data directly to/from RAM without routing through the CPU.<br/>• <b>Cycle Stealing Mode:</b> DMA controller takes control of the memory bus for 1 bus cycle to transfer 1 data word, then releases it back to CPU.<br/>• <b>Formula for Percentage CPU Slowdown:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>% CPU Time Stolen = (Device Transfer Rate / Bus Bandwidth) × 100%</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: If device prepares 1 byte every 4 µs and bus transfer takes 0.5 µs:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;CPU cycle stolen fraction = 0.5 µs / 4 µs = 12.5%.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DMA calculations, verify the units! Bus bandwidth and device preparation speed must be converted to the same time base (e.g. bytes per second) before dividing.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: DMA Cycle Stealing Percentage\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A hard disk transfers data at 2 MB/sec in cycle stealing mode. The system memory bus has a transfer speed of 10 MB/sec. What percentage of CPU cycle time is consumed by DMA?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Device data transfer rate = 2 MB/sec.<br/>• Step 2: Bus transfer bandwidth = 10 MB/sec.<br/>• Step 3: Percentage of CPU cycles stolen = (Device Rate / Bus Rate) × 100%.<br/>• Step 4: Calculation: (2 MB/s / 10 MB/s) × 100% = 0.2 × 100% = 20%.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ CPU cycle time consumed = 20%.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In vertical microprogramming, forgetting the +1 for the NO-OP state is the #1 student mistake in GATE!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In Booth\'s algorithm, shifting is ARITHMETIC shift right, never logical shift right (do NOT shift in a 0 on the left if the number is negative!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Shorthand Math Trick & Direct Highway Delivery\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Booth\'s Algorithm: Like calculating 99 × 6 by doing (100 - 1) × 6 = 600 - 6 = 594. Instead of adding 99 six times, you subtract once and add once!\n• DMA: Like a shipping company delivering pallets directly to the store warehouse without waiting for the CEO to personally inspect every single carton.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            11.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Cache Address Partitioning (Direct, Associative, Set-Associative)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Every memory address is partitioned into 3 fields:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Direct Mapped:       [ Tag Bits        | Line Bits (Index) | Block/Word Offset ]<br/>k-Way Set-Associative:[ Tag Bits        | Set Bits (Index)  | Block/Word Offset ]<br/>Fully Associative:   [ Tag Bits        | No Index Bits     | Block/Word Offset ]</div>• <b>Formulas to Calculate Field Widths:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Word / Byte Offset Bits:</b> <code>log2(Block Size in bytes)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Number of Cache Lines:</b> <code>Total Cache Size / Block Size</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Number of Sets:</b> <code>Total Cache Lines / k</code> (for k-way set associative).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Set Bits:</b> <code>log2(Number of Sets)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Tag Bits:</b> <code>Total Physical Address Bits - Set Bits - Word Offset Bits</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Tag Directory Size Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Tag Directory Size = (Total Cache Lines) × (Tag Bits + Valid Bit + Dirty Bit)</code>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Total cache capacity stores only DATA! The Tag Directory is extra hardware overhead: Tag Directory Size = (Number of Lines) × (Tag + 1 Valid + 1 Dirty bit)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            11.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Average Memory Access Time (AMAT) Formulas\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Model 1: Hierarchical Access (L1 checked first; on miss, lower level is visited):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = t_{hit_L1} + (Miss_Rate_L1 × Miss_Penalty_L1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If miss penalty goes to L2 cache: Miss_Penalty_L1 = t_{hit_L2} + (Miss_Rate_L2 × t_{mem}).<br/><br/>• <b>Model 2: Simultaneous Access (CPU queries Cache and Memory in parallel):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = (Hit_Rate × t_{cache}) + (Miss_Rate × t_{mem})</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Cache Write Policies:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Through:</b> Writes to both cache and RAM simultaneously. Safe, simple, but slow; needs Write Buffer.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Back:</b> Writes only to cache. RAM updated only when a modified block is evicted. Fast; requires <b>Dirty Bit</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unless an exam question explicitly says \'Simultaneous Access\', ALWAYS use the Hierarchical formula where Miss Penalty includes access time to the next lower level!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            11.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The 3 Cs of Cache Misses\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. Compulsory Miss (Cold Start):</b> First-ever access to a block. Unavoidable regardless of cache size.<br/>• <b>2. Capacity Miss:</b> Cache is physically too small to hold all active working set blocks. Occurs even in Fully Associative caches.<br/>• <b>3. Conflict Miss (Collision):</b> Multiple memory blocks map to the same set/line even though empty lines exist elsewhere.<br/>• <b>Key Insight:</b> Fully Associative cache has <b>ZERO conflict misses</b>!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Increasing associativity (e.g. 2-way to 4-way) reduces Conflict misses, but increases hit time because the tag comparator logic becomes wider and more complex.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Full Cache Address Splitting\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A 32-bit byte-addressable system has a 64 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 32-byte blocks. Find Tag bits, Set bits, Word offset bits, and Tag directory size (assume 1 valid bit).\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Word offset = log2(32 bytes) = 5 bits.<br/>• Step 2: Total lines = 64 KB / 32 B = 65536 / 32 = 2048 lines.<br/>• Step 3: Number of sets = 2048 lines / 4 lines per set = 512 sets.<br/>• Step 4: Set index bits = log2(512) = 9 bits.<br/>• Step 5: Tag bits = 32 - (9 + 5) = 32 - 14 = 18 bits.<br/>• Step 6: Tag directory size = 2048 lines × (18 tag bits + 1 valid bit) = 2048 × 19 bits = 38,912 bits = 4864 bytes.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Tag = 18 bits, Set = 9 bits, Offset = 5 bits, Tag Directory = 4864 bytes.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In word-addressable systems, word offset bits depend on word size (e.g. if 1 word = 4 bytes, 32-byte block holds 8 words ⇒ offset is 3 bits, not 5 bits!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Direct-mapped cache is simply a 1-way set-associative cache; fully associative cache has exactly 1 set.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Desk, Bookshelf & Public Library\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Registers: The pen and paper right in your hands (0 nanoseconds).\n• Cache: The small bookshelf right beside your desk (a few seconds).\n• Main Memory (RAM): The library across the college campus (10 minutes).\n• Secondary Storage (Disk): Ordering a book by postal mail from another state (days)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            12.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The 5-Stage Classic RISC Pipeline & Clock Cycle\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>The 5 Pipeline Stages:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>IF (Instruction Fetch):</b> Read instruction from Instruction Cache into IF/ID register.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>ID (Instruction Decode / Register Read):</b> Decode opcode and read source register operands.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>EX (Execute / Address Calculation):</b> ALU performs operation or computes effective memory address.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>MEM (Memory Access):</b> Read or write data from/to Data Cache (for LOAD/STORE).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>WB (Write Back):</b> Write computed result back into destination register.<br/><br/>• <b>Clock Cycle Period Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Clock Period (τ) = max(Stage Delays) + Pipeline Register Delay</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;The slowest stage sets the speed for all stages!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Pipeline clock cycle cannot be faster than the slowest stage! If stages take 100ps, 150ps, 250ps, 120ps, 110ps with 20ps buffer, Clock Period = 250 + 20 = 270ps.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            12.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The Three Classic Pipeline Hazards & Solutions\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. Structural Hazard (Hardware Resource Conflict):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Two instructions need the same hardware simultaneously (e.g. IF fetching instruction while MEM reads data from single-port RAM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> Separate Instruction Cache & Data Cache (Harvard Architecture).<br/><br/>• <b>2. Data Hazard (RAW - Read After Write):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Instruction 2 needs register result calculated by Instruction 1 before Inst 1 writes it back in WB stage.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> <b>Operand Forwarding (Bypassing)</b> feeds result directly from EX/MEM output to EX input without waiting for WB!<br/><br/>• <b>3. Control Hazard (Branch Instructions):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Target address not known until ID or EX stage, causing wrong instructions to be fetched.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> Branch Prediction, Branch Target Buffer, Delayed Branching.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In standard in-order pipelining, only RAW (True Dependency) causes pipeline stalls! WAR and WAW hazards only occur in out-of-order processors.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            12.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Pipeline Speedup & Effective CPI\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Execution Time:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Non-pipelined time for n instructions: <code>T_nonpipe = n × k × τ</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Pipelined time for n instructions: <code>T_pipe = (k + n - 1 + Stalls) × τ</code>.<br/>• <b>Speedup (S):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Speedup = T_nonpipe / T_pipe</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For large n (n → ∞) with no stalls: <b>Maximum Ideal Speedup = k</b> (number of stages!).<br/>• <b>Effective CPI:</b> <code>CPI = 1 + (Stalls per instruction)</code>. Actual Speedup = <code>k / CPI</code>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If 20% of instructions are branch instructions and each branch causes 2 stall cycles: Effective CPI = 1 + (0.20 × 2) = 1.4! Speedup over non-pipelined = k / 1.4.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Pipeline Speedup Calculation\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A 5-stage pipeline has stage delays of 2 ns, 1.5 ns, 3 ns, 2 ns, 1 ns. Register delay is 0.5 ns. In non-pipelined mode, an instruction takes 10 ns. Calculate the clock period and maximum speedup for a large number of instructions without stalls.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Clock period τ = max(2, 1.5, 3, 2, 1) + 0.5 = 3 + 0.5 = 3.5 ns.<br/>• Step 2: Non-pipelined time per instruction = 10 ns.<br/>• Step 3: Pipelined time per instruction for large n (ideal CPI = 1) = 1 × 3.5 ns = 3.5 ns.<br/>• Step 4: Speedup S = 10 ns / 3.5 ns = 2.857.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Clock period = 3.5 ns; Speedup = 2.86x.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Speedup is k ONLY when all stage delays are perfectly identical and register delay is 0!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Even with operand forwarding, a LOAD instruction immediately followed by an ALU instruction that uses the loaded value still requires 1 STALL cycle (Load-Use data hazard)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Laundromat Assembly Line\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Non-Pipelined: You wash load 1 (30 min), dry (30 min), fold (30 min), iron (30 min). Total = 2 hours per load. 4 loads take 8 hours!\n• Pipelined: As soon as load 1 finishes washing and enters the dryer, you drop load 2 into the washer! After a warm-up period, one complete load finishes every 30 minutes! Throughput increases 4x.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            13.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Pointers & Array Equivalence in C (Deep Tutorial)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. Visualizing Memory: How an Array is Stored in C:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Suppose you write: <code>int arr[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};</code> (assuming 4 bytes per integer):<br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Index:      arr[0]      arr[1]      arr[2]      arr[3]<br/>Value:        10          20          30          40<br/>Address:    [1000]      [1004]      [1008]      [1012]<br/>               ↑<br/>            arr (points here)</div>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The array name <code>arr</code> is a constant pointer:</b> It points to the first element: <code>arr == &arr[0] == 1000</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Because <code>arr</code> is a constant pointer, you <b>cannot reassign it</b> (<code>arr++</code> causes a compilation error!).<br/><br/>• <b>2. The Subscript Rule: Why does <code>3[arr] == arr[3]</code> work?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• In C, square brackets <code>[]</code> do not exist in machine code! C compiler translates <code>arr[i]</code> into:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>arr[i] ≡ *(arr + i)</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Pointer addition automatically scales by type size: <code>Address = Base + (i × sizeof(type))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Since addition is commutative (<code>a + b == b + a</code>), <code>*(arr + i)</code> is identical to <code>*(i + arr)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Therefore, in C: <code>arr[i] ≡ *(arr + i) ≡ *(i + arr) ≡ i[arr]</code>! Writing <code>3[arr]</code> gives 40!<br/><br/>• <b>3. Pointer Increments: <code>*p++</code> vs <code>(*p)++</code> vs <code>*++p</code>:</b><br/><div class=\'space-y-1.5 text-xs sm:text-sm my-2\'>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>*p++</code>:</b> Reads <code>*p</code> value first, then increments pointer <code>p</code> to point to next integer.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>(*p)++</code>:</b> Pointer <code>p</code> does NOT move! Increments the actual integer in memory by 1 (e.g. 10 becomes 11).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b><code>*++p</code>:</b> Advances pointer <code>p</code> to next slot first, then dereferences that new slot.</div>• <b>4. The Giant Trap: <code>arr + 1</code> vs <code>&arr + 1</code>:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>arr + 1</code> jumps <b>1 element (4 bytes)</b> ⇒ 1004.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>&arr + 1</code> jumps the <b>entire array of 4 ints (16 bytes)</b> ⇒ 1016!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When an array is passed into a function (like solve(arr)), it decays into a simple pointer! Inside the function, sizeof(arr) gives the size of a pointer (4 or 8 bytes), NEVER the array size!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            13.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Short-Circuit Evaluation & Side Effects in C\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>What is Short-Circuiting?</b> C guarantees left-to-right evaluation for logical operators and stops as soon as truth value is known.<br/>• <b>1. Logical AND (expr1 && expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>expr1 == 0 (False)</code>, the whole result MUST be False.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>expr2 is NEVER EXECUTED!</b> Any increments or assignments inside expr2 are completely skipped.<br/>• <b>2. Logical OR (expr1 || expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>expr1 != 0 (True)</code>, the whole result MUST be True.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>expr2 is NEVER EXECUTED!</b><br/><br/>• <b>GATE Code Trace Example:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-amber-300 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>int a = 1, b = 0;<br/>int res = (a-- || ++b);<br/>printf("%d %d %d", a, b, res);<br/>// Trace: a-- evaluates to 1 (True), then decrements a to 0.<br/>// Because 1 is True, || short-circuits! ++b is NEVER touched!<br/>// Output: 0 0 1</div>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In short-circuit expressions, watch out for skipped side effects! In (x || y++), if x evaluates to true, y++ is NOT executed, and y remains unchanged!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            13.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Storage Classes: static, auto, register, extern\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. auto:</b> Default for local variables. Stored on Call Stack. Created when block entered, destroyed when exited.<br/>• <b>2. static:</b> Stored in Data Segment (not stack!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Initialized only ONCE</b> when program loads (defaults to 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Retains its value across multiple recursive calls or function invocations.<br/>• <b>3. register:</b> Request to keep variable in CPU register. Address-of operator <code>&var</code> is ILLEGAL.<br/>• <b>4. extern:</b> References a global variable defined in another file without allocating new storage.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In recursion questions with static int count = 0;, the line static int count = 0; is executed ONLY ONCE on the very first call. On all subsequent recursive calls, it is skipped!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Tracing Pointer Expressions\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given: int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40}; int *p = a; What does *p++, (*p)++, *++p evaluate to step-by-step?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Pointer p starts at a[0] (value 10, address 1000).<br/>• Step 2: Execute *p++: Reads 10, then pointer p advances to a[1] (address 1004).<br/>• Step 3: Execute (*p)++: p points to a[1]. Reads 20, then increments the value inside a[1] from 20 to 21. p remains at a[1].<br/>• Step 4: Execute *++p: Pointer p advances to a[2] (address 1008) first, then reads value 30.<br/>• Step 5: Array is now {10, 21, 30, 40} and p points to a[2].\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Final array = {10, 21, 30, 40}; pointer p points to a[2] (30).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In C, sizeof(\'a\') is sizeof(int) = 4 bytes! In C++, sizeof(\'a\') is 1 byte (char).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Array boundary overflow in C does NOT throw an IndexOutOfBoundsException! It results in undefined behavior (silent memory corruption).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Russian Matryoshka Doll (Recursion)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Recursion: Opening a nesting doll to find a smaller doll inside. You keep unpacking until you hit the smallest solid doll (Base Case). Then you close them all back together (Call Stack unwinding)!\n• Array Decay: Handing someone a business card with your address written on it instead of physically moving your whole house to them.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            14.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Stack Infix to Postfix Conversion & Evaluation\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Why Postfix (Reverse Polish Notation)?</b> Computers evaluate postfix expressions in 1 linear pass without parentheses or backtracking.<br/>• <b>Postfix Evaluation Algorithm:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Scan expression from left to right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If operand (number): Push onto stack.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If operator: Pop two operands: <code>op2 = pop()</code> (right operand), <code>op1 = pop()</code> (left operand).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Calculate: <code>result = op1 (operator) op2</code>. Push result back onto stack.<br/><br/>• <b>Number of Valid Stack Permutations (Catalan Numbers):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total permutations obtainable using push/pop on n distinct elements:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Values: C_1 = 1, C_2 = 2, C_3 = 5, C_4 = 14, C_5 = 42.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In postfix evaluation, the FIRST popped element is the RIGHT operand, and the SECOND popped element is the LEFT operand: op1 - op2 (order matters for subtraction and division)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            14.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Circular Queue Boundary Conditions\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • In a linear array queue, dequeuing leaves dead space at the front that cannot be reused without shifting.<br/>• <b>Circular Queue Modulo Operations (Array Size N):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Enqueue(x):</b> <code>rear = (rear + 1) % N; queue[rear] = x;</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dequeue():</b> <code>front = (front + 1) % N; val = queue[front];</code><br/>• <b>Full Condition:</b> <code>(rear + 1) % N == front</code> (leaves 1 empty slot to distinguish from empty!).<br/>• <b>Empty Condition:</b> <code>front == rear</code>.<br/>• <b>Current Element Count:</b> <code>(rear - front + N) % N</code>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a circular queue of array size N that leaves 1 slot empty, the maximum capacity is (N - 1) elements!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            14.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Linked Lists & Floyd\'s Cycle Detection\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Floyd\'s Tortoise and Hare Cycle Finding:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Slow pointer moves 1 node per step; Fast pointer moves 2 nodes per step.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If a cycle exists, Fast will catch up and meet Slow inside the loop in O(N) time and O(1) space!<br/>• <b>Finding the Starting Node of the Cycle:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Once Slow and Fast meet, keep Fast at the meeting point.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Reset Slow to the <code>head</code> of the list.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Advance both pointers 1 step at a time. The node where they meet is the exact cycle entry point!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Reversing a singly linked list in-place requires O(N) time and strictly O(1) auxiliary space using 3 pointers: prev, curr, and next.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Evaluating Postfix with Stack\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Evaluate the postfix expression: 12 4 / 3 * 7 2 - +\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Push 12, Push 4. Stack: [12, 4].<br/>• Step 2: \'/\' arrives: op2 = 4, op1 = 12. Compute 12 / 4 = 3. Push 3. Stack: [3].<br/>• Step 3: Push 3. Stack: [3, 3].<br/>• Step 4: \'*\' arrives: op2 = 3, op1 = 3. Compute 3 * 3 = 9. Push 9. Stack: [9].<br/>• Step 5: Push 7, Push 2. Stack: [9, 7, 2].<br/>• Step 6: \'-\' arrives: op2 = 2, op1 = 7. Compute 7 - 2 = 5. Push 5. Stack: [9, 5].<br/>• Step 7: \'+\' arrives: op2 = 5, op1 = 9. Compute 9 + 5 = 14. Push 14. Stack: [14].\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Final evaluated value = 14.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In postfix conversion, operators with higher or EQUAL precedence on the stack must be popped first for left-associative operators!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In queue implemented using two stacks, amortized time per operation is O(1), but worst-case single dequeue can take O(N).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Plate Dispenser & Ticket Counter\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Stack (LIFO): The spring-loaded plate stack in a cafeteria. The last plate placed on top is the first one taken by a customer.\n• Queue (FIFO): A movie ticket line. The first person to arrive is the first person served.\n• Circular Queue: Like seats on a Ferris wheel—when the last seat passes, the wheel circles back to seat #1.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            15.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Binary Tree Mathematical Properties & Traversals\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Key Mathematical Theorems for Binary Trees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Leaf vs Degree-2 Nodes:</b> In any binary tree: <b>n_0 = n_2 + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(Number of leaf nodes is ALWAYS 1 more than the number of nodes with 2 children!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Max nodes at height h (root at h=0):</b> <b>2^(h + 1) - 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Strictly Binary Tree with n internal nodes:</b> Has exactly <b>2n edges</b> and <b>n + 1 leaves</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Tree Reconstruction from Traversals:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Preorder + Inorder:</b> Produces a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Postorder + Inorder:</b> Produces a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Preorder + Postorder:</b> CANNOT uniquely construct a general binary tree!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Inorder traversal MUST be given to reconstruct a unique binary tree! Without Inorder, left and right subtrees cannot be separated.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            15.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Binary Search Trees (BST) & AVL Balance Rotations\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>BST Inorder Property:</b> Inorder traversal of ANY valid BST ALWAYS yields keys in strictly <b>ASCENDING SORTED ORDER</b>.<br/>• <b>Number of distinct BSTs with n distinct keys:</b> Catalan Number <b>C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n)</b>.<br/>• <b>AVL Trees (Self-Balancing BST):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Balance Factor = <code>Height(Left Subtree) - Height(Right Subtree) ∈ {-1, 0, +1}</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If Balance Factor becomes ±2, rebalance via 4 rotations:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>LL:</b> Single right rotation.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RR:</b> Single left rotation.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>LR:</b> Double rotation (Left on child, Right on root).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RL:</b> Double rotation (Right on child, Left on root).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In any BST, the node with maximum value has NO right child, and the node with minimum value has NO left child!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            15.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Binary Heaps & Build-Heap O(n) Complexity\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Array Representation (1-based indexing for node at index i):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parent = <code>⌊i / 2⌋</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Left Child = <code>2i</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Right Child = <code>2i + 1</code>.<br/>• <b>Heap Complexities:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Find Min/Max: <b>O(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Insert key: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Extract Min/Max: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Build-Heap (from arbitrary array):</b> <b>O(n) linear time!</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Why O(n)? Most nodes are near leaves with tiny heights: ∑ (h / 2^h) converges to 2.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Building a binary heap takes O(n) time, NOT O(n log n)! In a Min-Heap of n elements, the maximum element is always at one of the leaf nodes (indices ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 to n).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Reconstructing Binary Tree\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given Preorder: [A, B, D, E, C, F] and Inorder: [D, B, E, A, F, C]. What is the Postorder traversal?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: First element in Preorder is Root = A.<br/>• Step 2: Find A in Inorder: Left subtree is {D, B, E}; Right subtree is {F, C}.<br/>• Step 3: In Preorder, next 3 items {B, D, E} belong to Left subtree ⇒ B is root of Left subtree.<br/>• Step 4: In Inorder, D is left of B, E is right of B.<br/>• Step 5: For Right subtree: Preorder has C then F; Inorder has F before C ⇒ C is root, F is left child.<br/>• Step 6: Compute Postorder (Left, Right, Root): D, E, B, F, C, A.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Postorder: D, E, B, F, C, A.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In a Max-Heap with n elements, finding the minimum element takes O(n) time, because you must scan all leaf nodes!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Deleting an arbitrary element from a heap takes O(log n) time provided its index is known; finding it takes O(n).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Corporate Hierarchy & Emergency Room Triage\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Binary Tree: Like a management chart where every manager has at most two direct reports (left and right vice presidents).\n• Priority Queue (Heap): Like an emergency room triage where doctors treat patients by severity of injury (priority), not arrival order!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            16.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Asymptotic Growth Hierarchy & Limits\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Standard Rate of Growth Hierarchy (Memorize for GATE!):</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-yellow-300 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>c < log(log n) < log n < (log n)² < √n < n < n log n < n² < n³ < 2^n < 3^n < n! < n^n</div>• <b>Testing Growth Rates with Limits:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Compute <code>L = lim_{n→∞} [ f(n) / g(n) ]</code>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = 0: f(n) grows strictly slower: <code>f(n) = o(g(n))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = constant (0 < L < ∞): <code>f(n) = Θ(g(n))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If L = ∞: f(n) grows strictly faster: <code>f(n) = ω(g(n))</code>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Polynomials always beat logarithms: (log n)^1000 = o(n^0.001) for sufficiently large n! Exponentials always beat polynomials: n^1000 = o(1.001^n)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            16.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Master Theorem for Divide-and-Conquer Recurrences\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • For recurrence <b>T(n) = a · T(n / b) + Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b> (where a ≥ 1, b > 1):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Compare <b>a</b> with <b>b^k</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Case 1 (a > b^k):</b> Root work dominates ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a))</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Case 2 (a = b^k):</b> Work balanced across levels:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p > -1: <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^{p+1} n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p = -1: <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log(log n))</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Case 3 (a < b^k):</b> Work at leaf level dominates ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n/log n: a = 2, b = 2, k = 1 ⇒ a = b^k, but p = -1! By Case 2 (p = -1): T(n) = Θ(n log(log n))!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Applying Master Theorem\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Solve T(n) = 8 T(n / 2) + n² log² n.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Identify: a = 8, b = 2, f(n) = n² log² n ⇒ k = 2, p = 2.<br/>• Step 2: Calculate b^k = 2² = 4.<br/>• Step 3: Compare a with b^k: a = 8 > 4 = b^k ⇒ Case 1!<br/>• Step 4: Exponent log_b a = log_2 8 = 3.<br/>• Step 5: Therefore, T(n) = Θ(n^3).\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ T(n) = Θ(n³).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Master Theorem cannot be applied if a is not constant (e.g. 2^n T(n/2)) or if b is not constant!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Master theorem for subtracting recurrences: T(n) = T(n - 1) + n gives Θ(n²); T(n) = T(n - 1) + 1 gives Θ(n).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Delivery Speed of Transport Modes\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Big-O: An upper bound promise. Like saying \'a flight from Delhi to Mumbai takes at most 3 hours\'. It might take 2 hours, but will never exceed 3 hours.\n• Big-Omega (Ω): A lower bound guarantee. \'Walking from Delhi to Mumbai will take at least 5 days\'.\n• Big-Theta (Θ): A tight bound. Both upper and lower bounds match.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            17.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Master Summary Table of Sorting Algorithms\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Complete Comparison Table for GATE CSE:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Algorithm      Best Time     Avg Time      Worst Time    Space     Stable?   Method<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Insertion Sort O(n)          O(n²)         O(n²)         O(1)      YES       Incremental<br/>Merge Sort     O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n)      YES       Divide & Conquer<br/>Heap Sort      O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(1)      NO        Selection/Heap<br/>Quick Sort     O(n log n)    O(n log n)    O(n²)         O(log n)  NO        Partitioning<br/>Counting Sort  O(n + k)      O(n + k)      O(n + k)      O(k)      YES       Non-comparison</div>• <b>Stability Definition:</b> Preserves the relative order of records with equal keys.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The theoretical lower bound for any comparison-based sorting algorithm in the worst case is Ω(n log n)! Non-comparison sorts (Counting, Radix) achieve O(n) by using array indexing instead of comparisons.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            17.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Hash Table Collisions: Chaining vs Open Addressing\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Separate Chaining:</b> Each slot holds a linked list. Load factor α can exceed 1.<br/>• <b>Open Addressing (All keys stored in table, α ≤ 1):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Linear Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h\'(k) + i) % m</code>. Suffers from <b>Primary Clustering</b> (long blocks of occupied slots).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Quadratic Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h\'(k) + c1·i + c2·i²) % m</code>. Eliminates primary clustering; suffers from <b>Secondary Clustering</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Double Hashing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i · h2(k)) % m</code>. Eliminates both clusterings (h2(k) must be relatively prime to m).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In open addressing, expected number of probes for unsuccessful search = 1 / (1 - α); for successful search = (1 / α) ln(1 / (1 - α))!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Double Hashing Probe Sequence\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Insert key 75 into a table of size 11 using h1(k) = k % 11 and h2(k) = 1 + (k % 10). If slot h1(75) is occupied, find the next probe slot.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Primary hash h1(75) = 75 % 11 = 9.<br/>• Step 2: Slot 9 is occupied ⇒ collision!<br/>• Step 3: Secondary hash h2(75) = 1 + (75 % 10) = 1 + 5 = 6.<br/>• Step 4: Next probe for i = 1: (h1(k) + 1 × h2(k)) % 11 = (9 + 6) % 11 = 15 % 11 = 4.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Next probe slot is index 4.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In QuickSort, if the array is ALREADY SORTED and the pivot is chosen as first or last element, QuickSort degrades to its WORST-CASE O(n²) time!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In hashing with open addressing, deleted slots must be marked with a special DELETED marker; setting to NULL breaks probe chains.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Playing Card Hand & Hotel Keycard Racks\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Insertion Sort: Like sorting cards in your hand—you pick each new card and slide it into its correct position among already sorted cards.\n• Hash Table: Like a hotel keycard rack. The room number directly maps to the hook without searching through all keys.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            18.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Greedy Method vs Dynamic Programming\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Greedy Method:</b> Makes locally optimal choice at each step without backtracking.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: Fractional Knapsack, Huffman Coding, Kruskal\'s, Prim\'s, Dijkstra.<br/>• <b>Dynamic Programming (DP):</b> Explores all possibilities by breaking into subproblems, but solves each subproblem only once.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Requires <b>Optimal Substructure</b> AND <b>Overlapping Subproblems</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: 0/1 Knapsack, Longest Common Subsequence (LCS), Matrix Chain Multiplication, Floyd-Warshall.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fractional Knapsack is solved by Greedy (sort by value/weight in O(n log n)). 0/1 Knapsack CANNOT be solved by Greedy; it requires Dynamic Programming in O(n × W) pseudo-polynomial time!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            18.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) Recurrence\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • For strings X of length m and Y of length n:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] == Y[j]</b>: <code>LCS[i, j] = 1 + LCS[i - 1, j - 1]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] ≠ Y[j]</b>: <code>LCS[i, j] = max(LCS[i - 1, j], LCS[i, j - 1])</code>.<br/>• <b>Time & Space:</b> O(m × n) time; space can be optimized to O(min(m, n)) using only two rows.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Matrix Chain Multiplication with n matrices, number of different parenthesizations is given by Catalan Number C_{n-1}!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Huffman Code Bit Length\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Frequencies: A: 45, B: 13, C: 12, D: 16, E: 9, F: 5. Construct Huffman tree and find total bits required.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Merge smallest two: F(5) + E(9) = 14.<br/>• Step 2: Merge next two smallest: C(12) + B(13) = 25.<br/>• Step 3: Merge 14 and D(16) = 30.<br/>• Step 4: Merge 25 and 30 = 55.<br/>• Step 5: Merge A(45) and 55 = 100.<br/>• Step 6: Code lengths: A = 1 bit; B, C, D = 3 bits; E, F = 4 bits.<br/>• Step 7: Total bits = (45×1) + (13×3) + (12×3) + (16×3) + (9×4) + (5×4) = 45 + 39 + 36 + 48 + 36 + 20 = 224 bits.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total encoded length = 224 bits.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Huffman code is a prefix code: No codeword is a prefix of another!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Huffman tree is always a strictly full binary tree with 2n - 1 total nodes for n characters.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Making Change & Remembering Past Math Results\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Greedy: Like a cashier giving change with the fewest coins by picking the largest coin possible first (25c, then 10c, etc.).\n• Dynamic Programming: \'What is 1 + 1 + 1 + 1? 4! Now add another + 1? 5!\' You didn\'t re-count 1 to 4 because you REMEMBERED the previous result (Memoization)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            19.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            BFS vs DFS Comparison & Applications\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Breadth-First Search (BFS):</b> Uses a <b>Queue</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds the <b>shortest path in unweighted graphs</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects bipartiteness (2-coloring).<br/>• <b>Depth-First Search (DFS):</b> Uses a <b>Stack / Recursion</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects cycles in directed graphs (via <b>Back Edges</b>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Computes <b>Topological Sort</b> of a DAG (reverse of finish times).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju\'s / Tarjan\'s algorithm).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DFS of a directed graph, a cycle exists IF AND ONLY IF there is at least one BACK EDGE! Forward and Cross edges do NOT indicate a cycle.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            19.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford vs Floyd-Warshall\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Dijkstra\'s Algorithm:</b> Single-Source Shortest Path for <b>non-negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time: <b>O((V + E) log V)</b> using min-heap. FAILS for negative edge weights!<br/>• <b>Bellman-Ford Algorithm:</b> Works with <b>negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Relaxes all edges (V - 1) times. Time: <b>O(V × E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects negative-weight cycles (if distance decreases on V-th relaxation).<br/>• <b>Floyd-Warshall Algorithm:</b> All-Pairs Shortest Path in <b>O(V³)</b> time using dynamic programming.<br/>• <b>Kruskal vs Prim (MST):</b> Kruskal uses edges (O(E log E) with DSU); Prim grows from a vertex (O(E + V log V)).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If all edge weights in a graph are distinct, the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) is 100% UNIQUE! Shortest path tree is NOT necessarily unique.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Kruskal\'s MST Edge Selection\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given edges sorted by weight: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (A-C, 2), (C-D, 3), (B-D, 4). Which edges are included in the MST?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Pick (A-B, 1) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.<br/>• Step 2: Pick (B-C, 2) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.<br/>• Step 3: Consider (A-C, 2) ⇒ A, B, C are already connected! Adding A-C creates cycle A-B-C-A. REJECT.<br/>• Step 4: Pick (C-D, 3) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.<br/>• Step 5: We have 4 vertices and 3 edges (V - 1 = 3). MST is complete!\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ MST edges: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (C-D, 3). Total weight = 6.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Dijkstra\'s algorithm can enter an infinite loop or give wrong answers if negative edge weights are present!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Topological sort exists IF AND ONLY IF the graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: GPS Navigation & Fiber-Optic City Network\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Dijkstra\'s Algorithm: How Google Maps finds the fastest driving route to your destination.\n• Minimum Spanning Tree: Laying down high-speed fiber cables between cities with the minimum total cost while ensuring every city is connected.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            20.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            DFA vs NFA: Equivalences, Power & State Bounds\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Equivalent Computational Power:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Every NFA can be converted into an equivalent DFA using <b>Subset Construction</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If an NFA has <b>n states</b>, the equivalent minimized DFA has at most <b>2^n states</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Key Distinctions in Transitions:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. In DFA: For every state and input symbol, there is <b>exactly ONE</b> deterministic transition: <code>δ: Q × Σ → Q</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. In NFA: There can be 0, 1, or multiple transitions, plus ε-transitions: <code>δ: Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) → 2^Q</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Dead / Trap States:</b> DFA requires explicit trap states for invalid input prefixes; NFA simply drops the branch.<br/><br/>• <b>State Minimization:</b> Done via Myhill-Nerode Theorem / Table-Filling Algorithm by grouping 0-equivalent, 1-equivalent, ..., k-equivalent states.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: DFA, NFA, and ε-NFA all have EQUAL computational power! They all recognize the exact same family: Regular Languages. Minimizing a DFA always produces a 100% UNIQUE minimal DFA!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            20.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Regular Expressions & Arden\'s Theorem\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Arden\'s Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If P and Q are regular expressions and P does NOT contain ε, then the equation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>R = Q + RP</b> has a <b>UNIQUE solution: R = QP*</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Identities to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>(r*)* = r*</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>(r + s)* = (r* · s*)* = (r* + s*)*</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>r · (s · r)* = (r · s)* · r</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>ε + r · r* = r* = ε + r* · r</code>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Arden\'s theorem R = Q + RP, P is on the RIGHT of R, giving R = QP*! If R = Q + PR (left recursion), the solution is P*Q.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            20.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Closure Properties of Regular Languages\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Regular Languages are CLOSED under ALL standard operations:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Union:</b> L1 ∪ L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Intersection:</b> L1 ∩ L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Complement:</b> L\' is Regular (swap final and non-final states in a complete DFA!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Difference:</b> L1 - L2 = L1 ∩ L2\' is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Concatenation:</b> L1 · L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Kleene Star:</b> L* is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Reversal:</b> L^R is Regular.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Regular languages are closed under ALL standard boolean operations! To complement a DFA, swap final and non-final states (make sure the DFA is complete with trap states first!).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Minimum DFA States for Suffix \'01\'\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Find the minimum number of states in a DFA accepting all binary strings ending with substring \'01\'.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Length of suffix pattern \'01\' is 2.<br/>• Step 2: Formula: For strings ending with a specific pattern of length k, minimum DFA states = k + 1.<br/>• Step 3: States = 2 + 1 = 3: q0 (start/empty), q1 (last bit 0), q2 (last bits 01 - Accept).<br/>• Step 4: Transitions from q2: on \'0\' go to q1; on \'1\' go to q0.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Minimum states = 3.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Swapping final and non-final states on an NFA does NOT complement the language! You must convert the NFA to a complete DFA first.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Pumping Lemma can prove a language is NOT regular; it can NEVER prove a language IS regular.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Soda Vending Machine State Controller\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Finite Automata: Like a soda machine. Inserting a coin transitions it from state \'Empty\' to \'Has 50c\'. Pressing the button dispenses soda and resets to \'Empty\'. It has finite memory—it only remembers its current state!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            21.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Deterministic PDA (DPDA) vs Non-Deterministic PDA (NPDA)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Crucial Distinction:</b> NPDA is <b>strictly more powerful</b> than DPDA!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- DPDA accepts <b>Deterministic Context-Free Languages (DCFL)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- NPDA accepts <b>Context-Free Languages (CFL)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Canonical Language Examples for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <code>L = { w c w^R }</code> has a marker \'c\' ⇒ <b>DCFL</b> (accepted by DPDA).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <code>L = { w w^R }</code> has NO marker (even palindrome) ⇒ <b>CFL, but NOT DCFL</b> (requires non-deterministic guess of midpoint!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <code>L = { a^n b^n c^n }</code> ⇒ <b>NOT CFL</b> (requires 2 stacks = Turing Machine).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unlike Finite Automata where DFA == NFA, in Pushdown Automata NPDA is STRICTLY MORE POWERFUL than DPDA: DCFL ⊂ CFL!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            21.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            CFL Closure Properties (The Famous Exceptions!)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>CFLs are CLOSED under:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Union, Concatenation, Kleene Star, Reversal.<br/>• <b>CFLs are NOT CLOSED under (Frequently tested in GATE!):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Intersection:</b> L1 ∩ L2 is NOT necessarily CFL!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Complement:</b> L\' is NOT necessarily CFL!<br/>• <b>Intersection with Regular Language:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If L is CFL and R is Regular, then <b>L ∩ R is ALWAYS CFL</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: CFL is NOT closed under Intersection or Complement! But DCFL is CLOSED under Complement (though NOT closed under Union or Intersection).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            21.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The Chomsky Hierarchy of Languages\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Type 3 (Regular):</b> Accepted by Finite Automata. Production: A → aB or A → a.<br/>• <b>Type 2 (Context-Free):</b> Accepted by Pushdown Automata. Production: A → α.<br/>• <b>Type 1 (Context-Sensitive):</b> Accepted by Linear Bounded Automata (LBA). Production: α → β where |α| ≤ |β|.<br/>• <b>Type 0 (Unrestricted / Recursively Enumerable):</b> Accepted by Turing Machine. Production: α → β.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strict hierarchy: Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ Recursive ⊂ Recursively Enumerable!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Intersection of Two CFLs\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> L1 = { a^n b^n c^m | n, m ≥ 1 } and L2 = { a^m b^n c^n | m, n ≥ 1 }. Are L1 and L2 CFLs? Is L1 ∩ L2 a CFL?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: L1 requires matching a\'s with b\'s (1 stack can do this) ⇒ L1 is CFL.<br/>• Step 2: L2 requires matching b\'s with c\'s (1 stack can do this) ⇒ L2 is CFL.<br/>• Step 3: Intersection L1 ∩ L2 = { a^n b^n c^n | n ≥ 1 }.<br/>• Step 4: Checking 3 counts simultaneously requires 2 stacks (Turing machine power).<br/>• Step 5: Therefore, L1 ∩ L2 is NOT a CFL!\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ L1 and L2 are CFLs, but their intersection is NOT a CFL.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Inherent ambiguity: Some CFLs are inherently ambiguous (every grammar for them is ambiguous, e.g. {a^i b^j c^k | i=j or j=k}).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          DCFLs are NEVER ambiguous! Every DCFL has an unambiguous grammar.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Balancing Scale & Parenthesis Matcher\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • PDA Stack: Like checking code syntax for matching curly braces {}. When { arrives, push onto stack. When } arrives, pop off stack. If stack is empty at end, code syntax is balanced!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            22.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Recursive (REC) vs Recursively Enumerable (RE)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Recursive Language (REC / Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Turing Machine halts on EVERY input (Halts and Accepts if w ∈ L; Halts and Rejects if w ∉ L).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Closed under ALL operations including Complement</b>: If L is REC, L\' is REC.<br/><br/>• <b>Recursively Enumerable Language (RE / Semi-Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Halts and accepts if w ∈ L.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If w ∉ L, may reject OR loop infinitely forever!<br/><br/>• <b>The Complement Duality Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>A language L is Recursive IF AND ONLY IF both L and its complement L\' are Recursively Enumerable!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If L is RE and L\' is also RE, then L is 100% DECIDABLE (Recursive)! If L is RE and undecidable, then L\' CANNOT even be RE!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            22.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Rice\'s Theorem for Turing Machines\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Rice\'s Theorem (Part 1):</b> Any <b>non-trivial semantic (language) property</b> of the language accepted by a Turing Machine is <b>UNDECIDABLE</b>.<br/>• <b>What is a Semantic Property?</b> A question about the LANGUAGE recognized by the TM (e.g. \'Is L(M) empty?\', \'Is L(M) finite?\', \'Is L(M) regular?\').<br/>• <b>Non-trivial means:</b> True for some TMs and False for other TMs.<br/>• <b>Syntactic Properties (Rice does NOT apply):</b> Questions about the machine structure itself (e.g. \'Does TM have 5 states?\', \'Does TM take 10 steps on input w?\') are DECIDABLE.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In GATE, if a question asks \'Is property P decidable for a Turing Machine?\' and P is about L(M) (like regularity, emptiness, finiteness, equivalence), the answer is INSTANTLY UNDECIDABLE by Rice\'s Theorem!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Applying Rice\'s Theorem\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Determine if the following problem is decidable: \'Given a Turing Machine M, does M accept a regular language?\'\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check if the property is semantic: \'Is L(M) regular?\' is a property of the language recognized by M (Semantic).<br/>• Step 2: Check if non-trivial: Some TMs accept regular languages, while others accept non-regular languages. Non-trivial!<br/>• Step 3: By Rice\'s Theorem, the problem is UNDECIDABLE.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Undecidable by Rice\'s Theorem.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          \'Does TM M take at most 100 steps on input w?\' is DECIDABLE because you can simulate it for 100 steps and stop!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Post Correspondence Problem (PCP) is undecidable for alphabet size ≥ 2, but decidable for unary alphabet (size 1).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Universal Antivirus Software Impossibility\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Halting Problem: Can anyone write an antivirus software that inspects any arbitrary code and guarantees whether it will eventually terminate or freeze in an infinite loop? Alan Turing proved it is mathematically IMPOSSIBLE for all programs!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            23.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Rules for Calculating FIRST Sets\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Definition:</b> FIRST(X) is the set of terminal symbols that appear as the first symbol of any string derived from X.<br/>• <b>The Three Golden Computation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. If X is a terminal: <b>FIRST(X) = { X }</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If X → ε is a production: Add <b>ε</b> to FIRST(X).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If X → Y1 Y2 ... Yk:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Add all non-ε symbols from FIRST(Y1) to FIRST(X).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If Y1 can derive ε, add all non-ε symbols from FIRST(Y2) to FIRST(X), and so forth.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If ALL Y1 ... Yk derive ε, add <b>ε</b> to FIRST(X).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Terminal symbols ALWAYS have FIRST(a) = { a }. When calculating FIRST(Y1 Y2), do not stop at Y1 if Y1 can produce ε!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            23.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Rules for Calculating FOLLOW Sets\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Definition:</b> FOLLOW(A) is the set of terminals that can appear immediately to the right of non-terminal A.<br/>• <b>The Three Golden Computation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. For Start Symbol S: Add <b>$ (End-of-input marker)</b> to FOLLOW(S).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. For production A → α B β:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <b>FIRST(β) except ε</b> is added to FOLLOW(B).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. For production A → α B OR A → α B β (where FIRST(β) contains ε):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <b>FOLLOW(A)</b> is added to FOLLOW(B).<br/>• <b>FOLLOW sets NEVER contain ε!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: FOLLOW set NEVER CONTAINS ε (epsilon)! If your calculation produces ε in a FOLLOW set, it is 100% WRONG!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Computing FIRST and FOLLOW Sets\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given grammar: S → A B; A → a | ε; B → b | ε. Compute FIRST(S) and FOLLOW(A).\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: FIRST(A) = { a, ε }; FIRST(B) = { b, ε }.<br/>• Step 2: For S → A B: FIRST(S) includes FIRST(A) minus ε = { a }. Since A → ε, include FIRST(B) = { b }. Since both A and B can produce ε, add ε. ⇒ FIRST(S) = { a, b, ε }.<br/>• Step 3: To find FOLLOW(A): Look at S → A B. Non-terminal B immediately follows A.<br/>• Step 4: Everything in FIRST(B) except ε goes into FOLLOW(A) ⇒ { b }.<br/>• Step 5: Since B derives ε, everything in FOLLOW(S) also goes into FOLLOW(A).<br/>• Step 6: S is start symbol ⇒ $ ∈ FOLLOW(S). Since S appears nowhere on RHS, FOLLOW(S) = { $ }.<br/>• Step 7: Therefore, FOLLOW(A) = { b, $ }.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ FIRST(S) = { a, b, ε }; FOLLOW(A) = { b, $ }.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In token counting: String constants like "Hello, World!" count as ONE single token!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Identifiers vs Keywords: In C lexing, keywords like \'int\', \'return\' are reserved and not identifiers.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Spell Checker & Sentence Grammar Parser\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Lexer: Reading characters and grouping them into valid vocabulary words (\'int\', \'sum\', \'100\').\n• FIRST set: Asking \'what words could legally start a sentence?\'.\n• FOLLOW set: Asking \'what word can legally appear immediately to the right of this word?\'.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            24.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            LL(1) Grammar Conditions & Disqualifiers\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • A grammar is <b>LL(1)</b> if and only if for every production A → α | β:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = ∅</b> (disjoint FIRST sets).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. At most one of α or β can derive ε.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If β derives ε, then <b>FIRST(α) ∩ FOLLOW(A) = ∅</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Instant Disqualifiers for LL(1):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Any grammar with <b>Left Recursion (A → A α)</b> is NEVER LL(1)!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Any grammar with <b>Ambiguity</b> is NEVER LL(1)!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Left recursion (immediate or indirect) INSTANTLY disqualifies a grammar from being LL(1)! Eliminate left recursion and left factoring before building LL(1) table.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            24.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Hierarchy and Power of LR Parsers\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Power Hierarchy (Strict Containment):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ CLR(1)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Number of Parsing States:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>States in LR(0) == States in SLR(1) == States in LALR(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- CLR(1) has significantly MORE states (splits states with different lookaheads).<br/><br/>• <b>Conflict Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Merging CLR(1) states to form LALR(1) <b>NEVER produces Shift-Reduce (SR) conflicts</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- It can ONLY introduce Reduce-Reduce (RR) conflicts.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Merging CLR(1) states into LALR(1) can NEVER introduce a new Shift-Reduce conflict! It can only introduce Reduce-Reduce conflicts.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Checking if Grammar is LL(1)\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Is grammar S → a S | a LL(1)?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check productions for S: α = a S and β = a.<br/>• Step 2: FIRST(α) = { a }; FIRST(β) = { a }.<br/>• Step 3: Check intersection: FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = { a } ≠ ∅.<br/>• Step 4: Since intersection is not empty, the parser cannot decide which rule to choose when lookahead is \'a\'.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Grammar is NOT LL(1) (requires left factoring to S → a S\' with S\' → S | ε).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Every SLR(1) grammar is an LALR(1) grammar, but not vice versa!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Every ambiguous grammar is neither LL(1) nor LR(k) for any k.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Predictive Keyboard & Assembling a Jigsaw Puzzle\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • LL(1) Parser: Like a predictive phone keyboard looking ahead by 1 letter to guess the next word without needing to delete and restart.\n• LR Parser: Assembling a jigsaw puzzle from individual bottom pieces upward until the entire picture is completed.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            25.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            S-Attributed vs L-Attributed SDT\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>S-Attributed Definition:</b> Uses ONLY <b>Synthesized Attributes</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Evaluated strictly bottom-up (postorder traversal).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Naturally evaluated during <b>LR parsing (bottom-up)</b> without modifying parse stack.<br/><br/>• <b>L-Attributed Definition:</b> Uses Synthesized attributes AND restricted Inherited attributes.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- An inherited attribute can depend only on:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Attributes of symbols to its <b>LEFT</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Inherited attributes of its <b>parent</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Evaluated during <b>Depth-First Search (left-to-right)</b> traversal.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every S-attributed SDT is automatically an L-attributed SDT! The reverse is NOT true: L-attributed includes inherited attributes that depend on left siblings.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            25.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Common Code Optimizations & Basic Blocks\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Leaders in 3-Address Code:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Statement 1 is a leader.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Target of any jump (GOTO) is a leader.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Statement immediately following any jump is a leader.<br/><br/>• <b>Optimization Types:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Folding:</b> Compute constant operations at compile-time: <code>x = 2 * 3.14</code> becomes <code>x = 6.28</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Propagation:</b> Replace variable with its known constant value.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dead Code Elimination:</b> Remove code whose result is never used.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Strength Reduction:</b> Replace expensive operations with cheaper ones: <code>x * 2</code> replaced by <code>x << 1</code> or <code>x + x</code>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strength reduction replaces an expensive operation with a cheaper one (e.g. multiplication replaced by addition in loops). It does NOT eliminate code!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Identifying Leaders and Basic Blocks\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given: (1) i = 1; (2) j = 1; (3) t1 = 10 * i; (4) t2 = t1 + j; (5) if t2 < 100 goto (3); (6) goto (1). How many basic blocks?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Statement (1) is a leader (first statement).<br/>• Step 2: Target of goto (3) is statement (3) ⇒ (3) is a leader.<br/>• Step 3: Target of goto (1) is statement (1) (already a leader).<br/>• Step 4: Statement following jump (5) is statement (6) ⇒ (6) is a leader.<br/>• Step 5: Leaders are (1), (3), and (6).<br/>• Step 6: Block 1: [(1), (2)]; Block 2: [(3), (4), (5)]; Block 3: [(6)].\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total Basic Blocks = 3.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In L-attributed SDT, actions can be placed anywhere, but they can only use values from elements to their LEFT!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Three-address code has at most 3 addresses per instruction (e.g. x = y op z).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Inheritance Tree & Unused Clutter Removal\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • S-attributed: Like kids calculating their allowance and reporting it UP to their parents (Bottom-Up).\n• L-attributed: Like parents distributing inheritance to children from left to right.\n• Dead Code Elimination: Throwing away broken, unused furniture from an attic so you never waste time cleaning it.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            26.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The fork() System Call & Process Tree\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <code>fork()</code> clones the calling process to create an exact child process.<br/>• <b>Return Value of fork():</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>0 to the Child process</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>Child\'s PID (> 0) to the Parent process</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>negative</b> on failure.<br/><br/>• <b>Number of Processes Created:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>fork()</code> is executed <b>n times</b> sequentially: Total processes = <b>2^n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of <b>Child processes</b> created = <b>2^n - 1</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In short-circuit expressions like fork() && fork(): the second fork executes ONLY in the parent process because fork() returns 0 (False) to the child, short-circuiting the &&!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            26.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            CPU Scheduling Metrics & Formulas\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Turnaround Time (TAT):</b> Completion Time - Arrival Time.<br/>• <b>Waiting Time (WT):</b> Turnaround Time - Burst Time.<br/>• <b>Response Time:</b> First Time CPU is Allocated - Arrival Time.<br/><br/>• <b>Algorithm Highlights:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>SJF / SRTF:</b> Provably gives <b>minimum Average Waiting Time</b> among all scheduling algorithms!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Round Robin (RR):</b> Time quantum q. If q is very large, RR becomes FCFS; if q is tiny, context switch overhead dominates.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Convoy Effect:</b> Occurs in FCFS when a long CPU-bound process blocks short I/O-bound processes.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SRTF (Shortest Remaining Time First) is preemptive SJF and gives minimum average waiting time, but can cause starvation for long processes!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Calculating Number of Created Processes\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> How many total child processes are created by: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { fork(); } ?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Loop runs 3 times, executing fork() 3 times unconditionally.<br/>• Step 2: Total processes = 2^n where n = 3 ⇒ 2^3 = 8 total processes.<br/>• Step 3: Total child processes = 2^n - 1 = 8 - 1 = 7 child processes.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total child processes created = 7.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Do not confuse total processes (2^n) with total child processes (2^n - 1)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Non-preemptive algorithms NEVER interrupt a process once it is running on the CPU.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Cooking on a 1-Burner Stove\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • CPU Scheduling: Like a chef with 1 stove burner (CPU) cooking 4 dishes (processes). FCFS finishes dish 1 completely before starting dish 2. Round Robin cooks each dish for 2 minutes, switching rapidly so no customer starves!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            27.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Critical Section Requirements & Counting Semaphores\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>The 3 Mandatory Requirements for Critical Section (CS):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Mutual Exclusion (Mandatory):</b> If Pi is in CS, no other process can enter CS.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Progress (Mandatory):</b> Only processes competing to enter CS decide who enters next.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Bounded Waiting:</b> A bound must exist on entries by others before a waiting process gets its turn.<br/><br/>• <b>Counting Semaphores Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Initialized to S0 (available resources).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Final Semaphore Value = S0 - (Wait operations) + (Signal operations)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If final value is negative (-k): <b>Exactly k processes are blocked in the waiting queue!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If counting semaphore is initialized to S0, after p wait operations and v signal operations, final value = S0 - p + v. If final value is negative (-k), exactly k processes are blocked in the waiting queue!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            27.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Deadlock: 4 Necessary Conditions & Banker\'s Algorithm\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>The 4 Coffman Conditions (Must ALL hold for deadlock):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Mutual Exclusion<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Hold and Wait<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. No Preemption<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Circular Wait<br/><br/>• <b>Banker\'s Algorithm:</b> Deadlock avoidance via Safe State analysis.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Need Matrix = Max Requirement - Current Allocation</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If available resources can satisfy Need of a process, it finishes and returns its allocation to Available.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An unsafe state does NOT mean deadlock! Every deadlocked state is unsafe, but an unsafe state may or may not lead to deadlock.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Counting Semaphore Value and Blocked Processes\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A counting semaphore S is initialized to 10. Then 14 wait() operations and 3 signal() operations are executed on S. What is the final value of S, and how many processes are blocked?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Formula: Final S = Initial + Signals - Waits.<br/>• Step 2: Final S = 10 + 3 - 14 = 13 - 14 = -1.<br/>• Step 3: When S is negative, the magnitude |S| represents the number of blocked processes.<br/>• Step 4: | -1 | = 1 process is blocked.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Final value of S = -1; Exactly 1 process is blocked.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Peterson\'s algorithm works strictly for TWO processes, not general n processes without modifications!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Strict alternation (using only turn variable) satisfies Mutual Exclusion, but FAILS Progress.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Single-Key Restroom & 4-Way Traffic Gridlock\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Semaphore: A single restroom key at a gas station. Only the person holding the key can enter (Mutual Exclusion). Others must wait until the key is returned (Wait/Signal).\n• Deadlock: A 4-way intersection gridlock where every car is blocked by the car in front of it, and no one can move forward!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            28.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Paging & Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with TLB\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Virtual Address Split: [ Page Number (p) | Page Offset (d) ]</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Page Offset = <code>log2(Page Size)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Page Number = Virtual Address Bits - Page Offset.<br/>• <b>Page Table Size:</b> <code>(Number of Pages) × (Page Table Entry PTE Size)</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with 1-level Paging:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>EMAT = Hit_Rate × (t_{tlb} + t_m) + (1 - Hit_Rate) × (t_{tlb} + 2 × t_m)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;On TLB Hit: 1 memory access (for data word).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;On TLB Miss: 2 memory accesses (1 for Page Table Entry + 1 for data word)!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a TLB hit, memory is accessed ONCE (for data). On a TLB miss in 1-level paging, memory is accessed TWICE (first for page table entry, second for data word)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            28.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Page Replacement Algorithms & Belady\'s Anomaly\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>FIFO (First-In First-Out):</b> Replaces the oldest loaded page.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Suffers from <b>Belady\'s Anomaly</b>: Increasing the number of page frames can INCREASE the number of page faults!<br/>• <b>LRU (Least Recently Used):</b> Replaces page not used for longest time in the past. (Stack algorithm; NEVER suffers from Belady\'s Anomaly).<br/>• <b>Optimal Page Replacement (OPT / MIN):</b> Replaces page that will not be used for longest time in the FUTURE. (Provably minimum page faults).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Belady\'s Anomaly occurs in FIFO! Stack algorithms like LRU and Optimal NEVER suffer from Belady\'s Anomaly.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            28.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Unix Inodes & Maximum File Size\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Disk Block Pointer Hierarchy in an Inode:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Direct Pointers (e.g. 12 pointers):</b> 12 × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Single Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size) × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Double Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size)² × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Triple Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size)³ × Block Size.<br/>• <b>Max File Size = Sum of all addressable bytes across direct, single, double, and triple pointers!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Number of pointers per indirect block = Block Size / Disk Pointer Size. For 1 KB blocks and 4-byte pointers: 1024 / 4 = 256 pointers per block!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: EMAT Calculation with TLB\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A system has TLB access time of 20 ns and main memory access time of 100 ns. The TLB hit ratio is 90%. What is the effective memory access time EMAT?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: On TLB hit: access TLB (20 ns) + access Memory for data (100 ns) = 120 ns.<br/>• Step 2: On TLB miss: access TLB (20 ns) + access Memory for Page Table (100 ns) + access Memory for data (100 ns) = 220 ns.<br/>• Step 3: EMAT = (0.90 × 120 ns) + (0.10 × 220 ns).<br/>• Step 4: EMAT = 108 ns + 22 ns = 130 ns.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ EMAT = 130 ns.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In multi-level paging with k levels: On TLB miss, you need k memory accesses for page tables + 1 memory access for data = (k + 1) memory accesses!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Internal fragmentation occurs on the last page of a process; paging completely eliminates external fragmentation.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Novel Chapter Index & Speed Dial\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Paging: Dividing a 500-page book into 10-page chapters so only the chapter you are currently reading needs to be open on your desk.\n• TLB: A smartphone speed-dial list storing your top 8 contacts so you don\'t look up their numbers in the full directory each time.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            29.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            ER to Relational Table Conversion Rules (Minimal Tables)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Converting Relationships into Minimal Tables:</b><br/><div class=\'font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed\'>Relationship Cardinality   Table Conversion Rule                                  Min Tables<br/>---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Many-to-Many (M:N)         Requires a separate relationship table (FKs of both)   3 tables<br/>One-to-Many (1:N)          Combine into \'Many\' side table using \'1\' side PK as FK 2 tables<br/>One-to-One (1:1)           Can merge into either table (or 1 table if total part) 2 (or 1)</div>• <b>Weak Entity:</b> Has no primary key of its own; uses discriminator and owner entity\'s primary key.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Weak entity and its identifying relationship are ALWAYS combined into <b>1 single table</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In an M:N relationship, you ALWAYS need a separate third relationship table! In a 1:N relationship, never create a separate relationship table—place the primary key of the \'1\' side as a foreign key on the \'N\' side!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            29.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Relational Algebra vs SQL Rules & NULL Logic\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Relational Algebra:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selection (σ):</b> Filters rows matching predicate.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Projection (π):</b> Selects columns and <b>AUTOMATICALLY ELIMINATES DUPLICATES</b>!<br/>• <b>SQL Duplicate Handling:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;SQL preserves duplicate rows by default! You must explicitly write <code>SELECT DISTINCT</code> to remove duplicates.<br/><br/>• <b>Three-Valued Logic in SQL (TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Comparisons with <code>NULL</code> (like <code>x = NULL</code> or <code>NULL = NULL</code>) evaluate to <b>UNKNOWN</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Rows where WHERE condition evaluates to UNKNOWN or FALSE are NOT returned.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: π in Relational Algebra eliminates duplicates automatically, but SELECT in SQL preserves duplicates unless DISTINCT is explicitly written! COUNT(*) counts rows including NULLs; COUNT(col) ignores NULLs.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Minimal Tables for ER Schema\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Entities E1, E2, E3. Relationship R1 between E1 and E2 is M:N. Relationship R2 between E2 and E3 is 1:N. How many minimal tables are required?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: E1 table is created.<br/>• Step 2: E2 table is created.<br/>• Step 3: M:N relationship R1 requires a separate relationship table (R1_table).<br/>• Step 4: 1:N relationship R2: E3 can store FK of E2 ⇒ No separate table for R2.<br/>• Step 5: E3 table is created (with FK referencing E2).<br/>• Step 6: Total tables: E1, E2, R1_table, E3 = 4 tables.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Minimum number of tables = 4.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In SQL, WHERE NULL = NULL returns 0 rows! To check for nulls, you must write \'WHERE col IS NULL\'.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          HAVING clause filters groups AFTER aggregation; WHERE clause filters individual rows BEFORE grouping.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: University Enrollment & Spreadsheet Database\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • ER Model: The architectural blueprint of a university database showing Students, Courses, and Departments.\n• Relational Algebra: Filter rows (Select σ), pick specific columns (Project π), and merge tables on common keys (Natural Join ⨝).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            30.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The Step-by-Step Normal Forms Checklist (1NF to BCNF)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1NF:</b> Every attribute contains strictly <b>atomic values</b> (no sets or lists).<br/>• <b>2NF:</b> Relation is in 1NF AND contains <b>NO Partial Dependency</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;(No non-prime attribute depends on a proper subset of any candidate key).<br/>• <b>3NF:</b> For every non-trivial FD <b>X → Y</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Either <b>X is a Superkey</b>,<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• OR <b>Y is a Prime Attribute</b> (part of some candidate key).<br/>• <b>BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form):</b> For every non-trivial FD <b>X → Y</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>X MUST BE A SUPERKEY!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 3NF, the RHS (Y) is allowed to be a prime attribute even if LHS (X) is not a superkey! In BCNF, this exception is REMOVED: LHS (X) MUST be a superkey!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            30.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Decomposition Guarantees: Lossless Join & Dependency Preservation\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Lossless Join Test (for binary decomposition R1, R2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Decomposition is Lossless IF AND ONLY IF common attribute is a superkey of at least one relation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>(R1 ∩ R2) → R1 &nbsp;OR&nbsp; (R1 ∩ R2) → R2</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Decomposition Guarantees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any relation can <b>ALWAYS be decomposed into 3NF</b> that is BOTH Lossless AND Dependency Preserving.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any relation can <b>ALWAYS be decomposed into BCNF</b> that is Lossless, but Dependency Preservation is NOT guaranteed!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: BCNF decomposition guarantees Lossless Join, but may LOSE functional dependencies! 3NF decomposition guarantees BOTH Lossless Join AND Dependency Preservation.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            30.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            B-Tree vs B+ Tree Node Structure & Search Order\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>B-Tree:</b> Keys and record pointers are stored in BOTH internal and leaf nodes.<br/>• <b>B+ Tree:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Data pointers are stored <b>ONLY in Leaf Nodes</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Leaf nodes are connected as a <b>linked list</b> for fast sequential range scans.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal nodes contain only routing keys and child pointers (higher fanout!).<br/>• <b>Node Capacities (Order p):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal node holds at most <b>p pointers</b> and <b>p - 1 keys</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimum keys in internal node: <b>⌈p / 2⌉ - 1</b> (except root: min 1 key).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In B+ Trees, internal nodes only store routing keys (no data pointers)! Hence internal nodes fit far more keys per disk block than standard B-Trees, keeping tree height very small (typically 3 to 4).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Finding Highest Normal Form\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Relation R(A, B, C, D) with FDs: { AB → C, C → D, D → A }. What is the highest normal form of R?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Compute candidate keys: (AB)+ = {A,B,C,D} ⇒ AB is a key. (BC)+ = {B,C,D,A} ⇒ BC is a key. (BD)+ = {B,D,A,C} ⇒ BD is a key.<br/>• Step 2: Candidate keys: AB, BC, BD. Prime attributes = { A, B, C, D } (ALL attributes are prime!).<br/>• Step 3: Check 2NF: No non-prime attributes exist ⇒ 2NF holds trivially!<br/>• Step 4: Check 3NF: For C → D, D is prime! For D → A, A is prime! Since RHS is always prime, 3NF holds!<br/>• Step 5: Check BCNF: In C → D, C is not a superkey. Fails BCNF!\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Highest normal form is 3NF.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If ALL attributes in a relation are Prime Attributes, the relation is GUARANTEED to be in 3NF!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Order of B-tree p means maximum child pointers; maximum keys is always p - 1.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Student Roll Number & Dictionary Thumb Index\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Functional Dependency: Like a student roll number uniquely determining their name, email, and branch (RollNo -> Name, Email, Branch).\n• B+ Tree: The alphabetical thumb-tabs on a 2000-page physical dictionary, letting you jump to \'Network\' in 3 flips instead of flipping through every single page!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            31.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Conflict Serializability & Precedence Graphs\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Two operations are in CONFLICT if:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. They belong to <b>different transactions</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. They access the <b>same data item</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>At least one of them is a WRITE</b> operation.<br/><br/>• <b>Precedence Graph (Testing Conflict Serializability):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Draw directed edge <b>Ti → Tj</b> if Ti executes a conflicting operation BEFORE Tj.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Testing Rule:</b> Schedule is Conflict Serializable <b>IF AND ONLY IF the precedence graph has NO CYCLES (Acyclic)!</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Equivalent serial order = <b>Topological Sort</b> of the graph.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If precedence graph contains a cycle (e.g. T1 → T2 → T1), it is 100% NOT conflict serializable! A schedule without cycles is always conflict serializable.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            31.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Two-Phase Locking (2PL) Protocol\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Basic 2PL:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Growing Phase:</b> May acquire locks, cannot release any lock.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Shrinking Phase:</b> May release locks, cannot acquire any new lock.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Guarantees Conflict Serializability!</b> But DOES NOT prevent deadlocks or cascading aborts.<br/><br/>• <b>Strict 2PL:</b> Holds all EXCLUSIVE (X) write locks until Commit/Abort. (Prevents cascading rollbacks!).<br/>• <b>Rigorous 2PL:</b> Holds ALL locks (shared and exclusive) until Commit/Abort.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Basic 2PL guarantees Conflict Serializability, but is NOT free from Deadlocks! Strict 2PL is Recoverable and Cascading-Free.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Conflict Serializability Cycle Detection\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given schedule S: r1(X), r2(Y), w1(Y), w2(X), c1, c2. Is S conflict serializable?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Conflict on X: r1(X) then w2(X) ⇒ T1 executes before T2 ⇒ Edge T1 → T2.<br/>• Step 2: Conflict on Y: r2(Y) then w1(Y) ⇒ T2 executes before T1 ⇒ Edge T2 → T1.<br/>• Step 3: Graph contains directed cycle T1 ⇄ T2.<br/>• Step 4: Since precedence graph has a cycle, S is NOT conflict serializable.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Schedule S is NOT conflict serializable.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Every conflict serializable schedule is view serializable, but not vice versa! View serializability allows blind writes.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          ACID: Durability guarantees that committed changes survive power failures using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: ATM Cash Withdrawal & Bank Balance Consistency\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Atomicity: If an ATM dispenses $100 cash but the machine loses power before updating your account balance, the system automatically rolls back so your money isn\'t lost.\n• Conflict Serializability: Making concurrent transactions execute as if they ran one after another in some clean serial order.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            32.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) Polynomial Division\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Let generator polynomial G(x) have degree <b>k</b> (i.e. k + 1 bits).<br/>• <b>Encoding Steps:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Append <b>k zeros</b> to the end of the original data frame.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Perform <b>Modulo-2 Binary Division</b> (using XOR instead of subtraction).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. The k-bit remainder is the <b>CRC Checksum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Transmitted frame = <b>Original Data + CRC Remainder</b>.<br/>• <b>Receiver Check:</b> Receiver divides received frame by G(x). If remainder is all 0s, frame is accepted.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If generator polynomial has degree k, append exactly k zeros to data! Modulo-2 division uses XOR (0⊕0=0, 1⊕1=0, 0⊕1=1, 1⊕0=1). Never use arithmetic borrowing!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            32.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Hamming Code & Error Correction\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Parity Bits Formula:</b> For m data bits and p parity bits:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>2^p ≥ m + p + 1</b>.<br/>• <b>Hamming Distance (d):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• To detect up to <b>d errors</b>: Minimum Hamming distance = <b>d + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• To correct up to <b>t errors</b>: Minimum Hamming distance = <b>2t + 1</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To correct t single-bit errors, minimum distance d_min ≥ 2t + 1! To detect t errors, d_min ≥ t + 1.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Calculating CRC Transmitted Frame\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given Data = 100100 and Generator polynomial G(x) = x³ + x² + 1 (binary 1101). Find the CRC remainder and transmitted bit sequence.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Degree of G(x) is 3 ⇒ Append 3 zeros to data: 100100 000.<br/>• Step 2: Divide 100100000 by 1101 using Modulo-2 XOR division.<br/>• Step 3: 3-bit remainder is 001.<br/>• Step 4: Transmitted codeword = 100100 001.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ CRC remainder = 001; Transmitted frame = 100100001.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In bit stuffing: Sender inserts a \'0\' after FIVE consecutive 1s! Flag is 01111110 (six 1s).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Physical layer deals with bits, Data Link with frames, Network with packets, Transport with segments.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Postal Mail Envelope & Barcode Checksum\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Layered Architecture: Writing a letter (Application), translating it to Spanish (Presentation), putting it in an envelope (Transport), writing address (Network), placing in delivery truck (Data Link), sending along highway (Physical).\n• CRC: The checksum barcode on the back of an envelope that detects if a digit got smudged in the rain.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            33.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Sliding Window Protocol Efficiency Formulas\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Key Parameters:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Transmission Time: <code>Tt = Frame Size (L) / Bandwidth (B)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Propagation Time: <code>Tp = Distance (d) / Velocity (v)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parameter a: <code>a = Tp / Tt</code>.<br/>• <b>Efficiency Formulas:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Stop-and-Wait:</b> <code>η = 1 / (1 + 2a)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Sliding Window with window size W:</b> <code>η = W / (1 + 2a)</code> (capped at 100%).<br/><br/>• <b>Window Sizes for k-bit sequence numbers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Go-Back-N (GBN):</b> Sender Window Ws = <b>2^k - 1</b>; Receiver Window Wr = <b>1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selective Repeat (SR):</b> Sender Window Ws = <b>2^(k - 1)</b>; Receiver Window Wr = <b>2^(k - 1)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Selective Repeat (SR), Ws + Wr ≤ 2^k. Since Ws = Wr, maximum window size is 2^(k-1)! In GBN, Ws ≤ 2^k - 1 and Wr = 1.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            33.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Size\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Collision Detection Condition:</b> Transmission time MUST be at least twice propagation time:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Tt ≥ 2 × Tp</b>.<br/>• <b>Minimum Frame Size Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>L_min ≥ 2 × Tp × Bandwidth</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;= <b>2 × (Distance / Velocity) × Bandwidth</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In CSMA/CD, L_min = 2 × Tp × Bandwidth! If frame is smaller than L_min, sender finishes transmitting before collision signal arrives, failing to detect collision.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Length\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A 1 km network has bandwidth 1 Gbps and signal velocity 2 × 10^8 m/s. Calculate the minimum frame size for CSMA/CD.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Distance d = 1000 m; Velocity v = 2 × 10^8 m/s.<br/>• Step 2: Tp = d / v = 1000 / (2 × 10^8) = 5 × 10^(-6) seconds = 5 µs.<br/>• Step 3: Bandwidth B = 1 Gbps = 10^9 bits/sec.<br/>• Step 4: L_min = 2 × Tp × B = 2 × (5 × 10^(-6)) × 10^9 = 10 × 10^3 = 10,000 bits = 1250 bytes.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Minimum frame size = 10,000 bits (1250 bytes).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In GBN, out-of-order packets are silently discarded because receiver window size is strictly 1!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In Selective Repeat, independent acknowledgments are sent for each packet, requiring memory buffers at receiver.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Walkie-Talkie & Crowded Dinner Party\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Stop-and-Wait: Like a walkie-talkie conversation. You say \'Over\' and wait until the other person acknowledges before speaking again.\n• CSMA/CD: Like people at a dinner party. You listen before speaking (Carrier Sense). If two speak at once (Collision), both stop, wait a random time (Backoff), and try again.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            34.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            IPv4 Subnetting & Usable Hosts\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • In CIDR notation <b>a.b.c.d / n</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Network bits = <b>n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Host bits = <b>32 - n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Total IP addresses = <b>2^(32 - n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Usable Host addresses = 2^(32 - n) - 2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(Subtract 2: First address is Network ID; Last address is Directed Broadcast Address).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a /30 subnet: 2^(32-30) = 4 IPs. Minus 2 = exactly 2 usable host addresses (ideal for point-to-point router links)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            34.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Fragmentation Offset Calculation Rules\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Key Fragmentation Header Fields:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>MF (More Fragments) Flag:</b> 1 for all fragments EXCEPT the last fragment (MF = 0 for last).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>DF (Don\'t Fragment) Flag:</b> If 1 and packet exceeds MTU, packet is dropped.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Fragment Offset:</b> Offset field in IPv4 header is measured in units of <b>8 bytes</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Offset = (Byte offset of data) / 8</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Data payload in each fragment (except last) MUST be a multiple of 8 bytes!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fragment offset is measured in units of 8 BYTES! Subtract the 20-byte IP header first: if MTU = 1500 bytes, max data payload is 1480 bytes (1480 / 8 = 185, a valid multiple of 8)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: IPv4 Packet Fragmentation\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> An IP datagram of 4000 bytes (including 20-byte IP header) arrives at a router where MTU = 1500 bytes. Find payload size, MF flag, and offset for each fragment.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Total data = 4000 - 20 = 3980 bytes.<br/>• Step 2: Max payload per fragment = 1500 - 20 = 1480 bytes (multiple of 8).<br/>• Step 3: Frag 1: Payload = 1480 bytes, Offset = 0 / 8 = 0, MF = 1.<br/>• Step 4: Frag 2: Payload = 1480 bytes, Offset = 1480 / 8 = 185, MF = 1.<br/>• Step 5: Remaining data = 3980 - 2960 = 1020 bytes.<br/>• Step 6: Frag 3: Payload = 1020 bytes, Offset = 2960 / 8 = 370, MF = 0.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Frag 1: 1480B, MF=1, Off=0; Frag 2: 1480B, MF=1, Off=185; Frag 3: 1020B, MF=0, Off=370.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Reassembly of fragments is performed ONLY at the DESTINATION host, never at intermediate routers!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Distance Vector routing suffers from Count-to-Infinity problem; solved by Split Horizon and Poison Reverse.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Slicing a Pizza & Oversized Cargo Disassembly\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Subnetting: Slicing a giant pizza into smaller slices so each office department gets exactly what they need without wasting slices.\n• Fragmentation: Shipping a sofa that cannot fit through the front door (MTU), so you take it apart into 3 pieces and reassemble inside!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            35.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            TCP Congestion Window Phases\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. Slow Start Phase:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Congestion Window (cwnd) starts at 1 MSS (Maximum Segment Size).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For every ACK received, cwnd increases by 1 MSS (effectively <b>DOUBLES every RTT</b>: exponential growth).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Continues until cwnd reaches <b>ssthresh (slow-start threshold)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>2. Congestion Avoidance Phase:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- cwnd increases by <b>1 MSS per RTT</b> (additive increase: linear growth).<br/><br/>• <b>3. On Loss Event:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Timeout (Severe Loss):</b> <b>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</b>, and <b>cwnd resets to 1 MSS</b> (re-enters Slow Start).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>3 Duplicate ACKs (Mild Loss - Fast Retransmit):</b> <b>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</b>, and <b>cwnd = ssthresh</b> (Fast Recovery).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On 3 duplicate ACKs, cwnd drops to ssthresh (not 1 MSS!) and enters Congestion Avoidance. On Timeout, cwnd resets all the way down to 1 MSS!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            35.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Application Layer Protocols Summary\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>DNS (Port 53):</b> Uses <b>UDP</b> for standard queries (fast); uses <b>TCP</b> for zone transfers.<br/>• <b>HTTP (Port 80) / HTTPS (Port 443):</b> Uses <b>TCP</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Non-persistent HTTP/1.0: 2 RTTs per object.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Persistent HTTP/1.1: 1 RTT per object after initial connection handshake.<br/>• <b>Email Protocols:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>SMTP (Port 25):</b> Push protocol (client to mail server, server to server).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>POP3 (Port 110) / IMAP (Port 143):</b> Pull protocols (client downloading mail from server).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SMTP is a PUSH protocol! POP3 and IMAP are PULL protocols! DNS uses UDP for queries but TCP for zone transfers.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Tracing TCP cwnd Growth\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A TCP connection has ssthresh = 16 KB and MSS = 2 KB. Starting from cwnd = 2 KB, trace cwnd after 5 RTTs (no loss).\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Round 0: cwnd = 2 KB.<br/>• Step 2: RTT 1 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 4 KB.<br/>• Step 3: RTT 2 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 8 KB.<br/>• Step 4: RTT 3 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 16 KB (reaches ssthresh).<br/>• Step 5: RTT 4 (Congestion Avoidance): cwnd increases linearly by 1 MSS (+2 KB) = 18 KB.<br/>• Step 6: RTT 5 (Congestion Avoidance): cwnd increases by 1 MSS (+2 KB) = 20 KB.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ After 5 RTTs, cwnd = 20 KB.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          TCP uses byte sequence numbers, NOT packet sequence numbers! If packet carries 100 bytes starting at seq 500, next packet has seq 600.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Silently discarding packets by UDP provides zero flow or congestion control.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Highway Speed Limit & Highway Traffic Jam\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • TCP Slow Start: Starting your car gently and doubling speed every block until you reach the threshold speed limit.\n• Congestion Avoidance: Once past the threshold, increasing speed by only 1 km/h each block to avoid crashing into traffic ahead!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            36.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Time, Speed, Distance & Relative Speed\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Fundamental Formula:</b> <b>Distance = Speed × Time</b>.<br/>• <b>Unit Conversion:</b> <b>1 km/h = 5 / 18 m/s</b> &nbsp;and&nbsp; 1 m/s = 18 / 5 km/h.<br/>• <b>Average Speed:</b> <b>Total Distance / Total Time</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If equal distances covered at speeds v1 and v2: <b>Average Speed = 2 · v1 · v2 / (v1 + v2)</b> (Harmonic Mean!).<br/>• <b>Relative Speed:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>Opposite directions:</b> Relative Speed = <b>v1 + v2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>Same direction:</b> Relative Speed = <b>|v1 - v2|</b>.<br/>• <b>Boats and Streams:</b> Downstream speed = <b>u + v</b>; Upstream speed = <b>u - v</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Average speed is NEVER the simple arithmetic mean (v1 + v2)/2! For equal distance intervals, average speed is always 2·v1·v2 / (v1 + v2).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            36.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Time & Work (The LCM Efficiency Method)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>LCM Method:</b> Assume Total Work = <b>LCM of all individual completion times</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Calculate 1-day efficiency = Total Work / Individual Days.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Combined efficiency = Sum of individual efficiencies.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Total Days = Total Work / Combined Efficiency.<br/>• <b>Pipes and Cisterns:</b> Inlet pipe adds positive work (+); Outlet pipe subtracts negative work (-).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Using the LCM method eliminates messy fractions completely! If A takes 12 days and B takes 18 days: Total Work = LCM(12, 18) = 36 units. Efficiency of A = 3, B = 2. Together = 36 / 5 = 7.2 days!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Two Trains Crossing Each Other\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Two trains of lengths 120 m and 180 m travel in opposite directions at 40 km/h and 50 km/h. How many seconds do they take to pass each other?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Total distance = Sum of train lengths = 120 + 180 = 300 meters.<br/>• Step 2: Relative speed in opposite directions = 40 + 50 = 90 km/h.<br/>• Step 3: Convert speed to m/s: 90 × (5 / 18) = 25 m/s.<br/>• Step 4: Time taken = Distance / Speed = 300 m / (25 m/s) = 12 seconds.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Time taken = 12 seconds.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          When a train crosses a pole or person, distance covered = length of train alone (pole has negligible width).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Successive percentage discount of x% and y% is NOT (x + y)%! Equivalent discount = x + y - (xy / 100)%.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Two Taps Filling a Bathtub\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Work and Time: Tap A fills a tub in 10 minutes (rate = 1/10 per min); Tap B fills it in 15 minutes (rate = 1/15 per min). Working together, their speeds add up: 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/6, filling the tub in 6 minutes!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            37.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Clock Angle Formula\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Angle between Hour and Minute hand at H hours and M minutes:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Angle θ = | 30 × H - (11 / 2) × M |</b>.<br/>• <b>Speeds of Hands:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Minute hand speed: <b>6° per minute</b> (360° / 60 min).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Hour hand speed: <b>0.5° per minute</b> (30° / 60 min).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Relative speed: <b>5.5° per minute</b>.<br/>• <b>Coincidence (0°):</b> Hands coincide <b>22 times in 24 hours</b> (not 24 times, due to 11-12-1 overlap).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Use θ = |30H - 5.5M|! If angle is greater than 180°, reflex angle is 360° - θ.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            37.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Painted Cube Slicing Formulas\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • A large cube painted on all outside faces is cut into <b>n³ smaller equal cubes</b> (n cuts per edge):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>3 Faces Painted (Corners):</b> ALWAYS exactly <b>8 cubes</b> (for any n ≥ 2).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>2 Faces Painted (Edges):</b> <b>12 × (n - 2)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>1 Face Painted (Face Centers):</b> <b>6 × (n - 2)²</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>0 Faces Painted (Inner Core):</b> <b>(n - 2)³</b>.<br/>• <b>Verification:</b> 8 + 12(n - 2) + 6(n - 2)² + (n - 2)³ = n³.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Regardless of the value of n, cubes with 3 faces painted is ALWAYS 8 (the 8 corners of the cube)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Angle between Clock Hands\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Find the angle between the hour hand and minute hand of a clock at 3:40.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Here H = 3, M = 40.<br/>• Step 2: Formula: θ = | 30 × H - (11 / 2) × M |.<br/>• Step 3: 30 × 3 = 90; (11 / 2) × 40 = 220.<br/>• Step 4: θ = | 90 - 220 | = | -130 | = 130°.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Angle between hands = 130°.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In calendar problems, remember that century years like 1900 are NOT leap years unless divisible by 400 (2000 is a leap year)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In blood relations, never assume gender from a person\'s name unless explicitly stated or implied by relationship.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Rubik\'s Cube Slicing & Wall Clock Hands\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Painted Cube: Like a 3x3x3 Rubik\'s cube painted red on outside. Only the 8 corner pieces have 3 painted faces! Center pieces inside the cube have 0 painted faces.\n• Clock Hands: At 12:00 both hands point straight up. By 12:30, the hour hand has already crept halfway toward 1 (15 degrees)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            38.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Subject-Verb Agreement (Concord) Rules\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Rule 1: Either/Or & Neither/Nor:</b> Verb agrees with the <b>CLOSER subject</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: \'Neither the manager nor the <i>employees were</i> present.\'<br/>• <b>Rule 2: As well as / Together with / Along with:</b> Verb agrees with the <b>FIRST subject</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: \'The <i>captain</i>, along with his crew members, <b>was</b> honored.\'<br/>• <b>Rule 3: Each, Every, Everyone, Neither (alone):</b> Strictly <b>SINGULAR</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: \'Each of the students <b>has</b> completed the project.\'\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In \'A as well as B\', verb follows A! In \'Either A or B\', verb follows B (the nearest subject)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            38.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Common Word Confusions in GATE\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Affect vs Effect:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Affect (Verb):</b> To influence. (\'The weather affected his mood\').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Effect (Noun):</b> The result/consequence. (\'The policy had a good effect\').<br/>• <b>Complement vs Compliment:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Complement:</b> Completes or enhances something (\'Bread and butter complement each other\').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Compliment:</b> An expression of praise (\'He paid her a nice compliment\').<br/>• <b>Principal vs Principle:</b> Principal = head of school / main; Principle = fundamental truth / rule.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Affect is almost always an Action (Verb), Effect is an End-result (Noun). Remember RAVEN: Remember Affect Verb Effect Noun!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Subject-Verb Agreement Selection\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Choose the correct verb: \'Neither the professor nor his research students _____ (was / were) able to solve the puzzle.\'\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Identify conjunction: \'Neither ... nor ...\'.<br/>• Step 2: Rule of Proximity: The verb agrees with the subject closer to it.<br/>• Step 3: The closer subject is \'his research students\' (Plural).<br/>• Step 4: Plural subject requires plural verb \'were\'.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Correct sentence: \'Neither the professor nor his research students were able to solve the puzzle.\'</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In reading comprehension, answers that use extreme words like \'always\', \'never\', \'only\' are usually wrong unless explicitly in the text!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          An assumption is an UNSTATED premise required for the conclusion to hold true.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Orchestra Conductor & Courtroom Evidence\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Concord: The subject and verb must play in harmony (Singular subject takes singular verb).\n• Critical Reasoning: Distinguishing between a confirmed fact stated in a text versus an unproven assumption made by the author.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>'}
